#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167292 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1800471e-9188-5bb1-9aed-d377de1f651c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315257}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186315257} :answer-field/id #uuid "ce831323-8c63-59e9-8d6f-c9bf55f74094" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317035}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "66845167-3565-541d-84d8-7b2d1bbcac26" :question/math-academy-id "q-342496" :question/problem "$$\nF(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}, & x\\in [-3,0] \\\\ -{x}^{2}, & x\\in (0,3)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsider the function $F(x)$ shown above. Find the value to which the Fourier series of the $6$-periodic extension $f(x)$ of $F(x)$ is converging when $x=2.$\n\nThe Fourier series converges to {{field-1}} when $x=2.$" :question/worked-solution "Suppose that $f(x)$ is a $2L$-periodic piecewise smooth function with the corresponding Fourier series\n\n$$\nS(x)=\\frac{{a}_{0}}{2}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi }{L}x)+{b}_{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi }{L}x).\n$$\n\nThen, the series converges for all $x\\in R.$\n\n- If $f(x)$ is continuous at $x,$ then the series converges to the value of the function at this point:\n\n$$\nS(x)=f(x)\n$$\n\n- If $f(x)$ has a jump discontinuity at $x,$ then the series converges to the midpoint between the values of left- and right-sided limits of the function at this point:\n\n$$\nS(x)=\\frac{{f}^{-}(x)+{f}^{+}(x)}{2}\n$$\n\nwhere ${f}^{-}(x)$ and ${f}^{-}(x)$ are the left- and right-sided limits of the function at this point, respectively.\n\nNotice that the $6$-periodic extension $f(x)$ of our function $F(x)$ is continuous at $x=2.$ \n\nTherefore, the Fourier series of $f(x)$ converges to \n\n$$\nf(2)=-{2}^{2}=-4.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167293 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "735db7a5-7bcb-5fa9-a24e-2f32aa107ab9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186511023}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186511023} :answer-field/id #uuid "790c545a-a448-5b09-a252-ea7895e22766" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186164979}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "ddf2fa7b-cdfe-5039-9760-76db03b3d4cb" :question/math-academy-id "q-342505" :question/problem "$$\nF(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}0, & x\\in [-\\pi ,0] \\\\ 1, & x\\in (0,\\pi )\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsider the function $F(x)$ shown above. Find the value to which the Fourier series of the $2\\pi $-periodic extension $f(x)$ of $F(x)$ converges when $x=\\pi .$\n\nThe Fourier series converges to {{field-1}} when $x=\\pi .$" :question/worked-solution "Suppose that $f(x)$ is a $2L$-periodic piecewise smooth function with the corresponding Fourier series\n\n$$\nS(x)=\\frac{{a}_{0}}{2}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi }{L}x)+{b}_{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi }{L}x).\n$$\n\nThen, the series converges for all $x\\in R.$\n\n- If $f(x)$ is continuous at $x,$ then the series converges to the value of the function at this point:\n\n$$\nS(x)=f(x)\n$$\n\n- If $f(x)$ has a jump discontinuity at $x,$ then the series converges to the midpoint between the values of left- and right-sided limits of the function at this point:\n\n$$\nS(x)=\\frac{{f}^{-}(x)+{f}^{+}(x)}{2}\n$$\n\nwhere ${f}^{-}(x)$ and ${f}^{-}(x)$ are the left- and right-sided limits of the function at this point, respectively.\n\nNotice that the $2\\pi $-periodic extension $f(x)$ of our function $F(x)$ is not continuous at $x=\\pi .$ The one-sided limits at this point are the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{-}(\\pi ) & =\\lim_{x\\to {\\pi }^{-}}f(x)=1 \\\\ {f}^{+}(\\pi ) & =\\lim_{x\\to {\\pi }^{+}}f(x)=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, the Fourier series of $f(x)$ converges to \n\n$$\n\\frac{{f}^{-}(\\pi )+{f}^{+}(\\pi )}{2}=\\frac{1+0}{2}=\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167294 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "319ba617-932b-5e50-ad59-7f9fc5569358" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-1)^{n}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315405} {:answer/id #uuid "4e80f0e0-0111-5ab1-af31-72ce58be7df0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-1)^{n+1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315566} {:answer/id #uuid "5124fd40-8d9d-59d1-b9d5-462811e8a2b7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315581} {:answer/id #uuid "81aaa659-9f4c-5eaa-8eb9-258c34266d04" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315834}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186315566} :answer-field/id #uuid "092a700c-e5f8-5d00-87b2-e2b8efc4840a" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316573} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6966166c-739e-51c2-95ac-07426c73734c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "discontinuous" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315713} {:answer/id #uuid "6c6da78c-e7d7-588b-9dbc-6b7138216a8e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "continuous" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315729}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186315729} :answer-field/id #uuid "74cef797-f0ca-5d6b-a7a0-8cd0b50fc3ad" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316825} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ac288ede-6270-5122-8d75-5c564dea62e5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316089} {:answer/id #uuid "bb57ee0f-4e98-51b8-8156-6f9c9902ebff" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316177} {:answer/id #uuid "f78cb4b7-2c3f-5897-a653-6458243d8bf1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316501}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186316089} :answer-field/id #uuid "7c0ad751-f851-5886-ae90-2055f3078408" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316844} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "05786c7a-92dc-57fc-b48d-bb19a624cf72" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315146} {:answer/id #uuid "bf5552f4-2beb-5387-9e82-f554df66605c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{\\pi }^{2}}{8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316197} {:answer/id #uuid "c9280989-590b-5f34-8860-61a1242ded3d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316252} {:answer/id #uuid "f4145436-6939-5a91-a9e8-a3eea1086a4a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{\\pi }^{2}}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316479}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186316252} :answer-field/id #uuid "9e296a53-e86f-5f70-b577-9511cf24a3b7" :answer-field/key "field-4" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316908}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "fb0887d0-dec5-559c-9512-f43e4278a2c9" :question/math-academy-id "q-342571" :question/problem "$$\nF(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}0, & x\\in [-\\pi ,0] \\\\ 1, & x\\in (0,\\pi )\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsider the function $F(x)$ shown above. The Fourier series of the $2\\pi $-periodic extension $f(x)$ of $F(x)$ is given by\n\n$$\nf(x)∼\\frac{1}{2}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{2}{\\pi (2n-1)}\\operatorname{sin}⁡((2n-1)x).\n$$\n\nSelect the correct options in the reasoning below.\n\nAt $x=\\frac{\\pi }{2},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}⁡((2n-1)\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{2})={{field-1}}.\n$$\n\nSince $f(x)$ is \n{{field-2}}\nat $x=\\frac{\\pi }{2},$ the corresponding Fourier series converges to \n\n$$\nf\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)={{field-3}}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have \n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{(-1)^{n+1}}{2n-1}={{field-4}}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, we rewrite our Fourier series as\n\n$$\nf(x)∼\\frac{1}{2}+\\frac{2}{\\pi }{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{2n-1}\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡((2n-1)x).\n$$\n\nNow, notice that at $x=\\frac{\\pi }{2},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}⁡((2n-1)\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{2})=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(-\\frac{\\pi }{2}+n\\pi )=(-1)^{n+1}.\n$$\n\nSince $f(x)$ is $\\text{continuous}$ at $x=\\frac{\\pi }{2},$ the corresponding Fourier series converges to \n\n$$\nf\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)=1.\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right) & =\\frac{1}{2}+\\frac{2}{\\pi }{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{2n-1}\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡((2n-1)\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{2}) \\\\ 1 & =\\frac{1}{2}+\\frac{2}{\\pi }{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{2n-1}\\cdot (-1)^{n+1} \\\\ \\frac{1}{2} & =\\frac{2}{\\pi }{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{(-1)^{n+1}}{2n-1} \\\\ {\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{(-1)^{n+1}}{2n-1} & =\\frac{\\pi }{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167304 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0e9e8e21-b550-5dab-acbe-bf94504b1d71" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{1}_{\\{2,4,6,8\\}}(B)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186553901} {:answer/id #uuid "23fcb709-e1df-5686-a023-45de9c7332ca" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{1}_{5}(B)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186553932} {:answer/id #uuid "6acf5d37-f233-58a7-b644-4950c5f08b96" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{1}_{5}(2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186554078} {:answer/id #uuid "6ec50b2b-0fb1-535c-bee6-07f2da381e05" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{1}_{B}(6)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186554088} {:answer/id #uuid "fd7b8b0c-b17c-5fc2-aad1-cf9ecfd4c52f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{1}_{B}(5)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186554420}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186554420} :answer-field/id #uuid "68354444-5465-5b9a-9ef0-8c6ea884ef5c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186165033}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "6df0b1a8-9045-561a-b52c-2dfb375e5b28" :question/math-academy-id "q-349167" :question/problem "Let $B=\\{2,4,6,8\\}.$ Which of the following is equivalent to $1[5\\in B]?$" :question/worked-solution "The indicator function $1[P]$ equals $1$ if the predicate $P$ is true and $0$ if $P$ is false.\n\n$$\n1[P]=\\{\\begin{aligned}1, & \\text{if }P\\text{ is true}, \\\\ 0, & \\text{if }P\\text{ is false}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nRecall that the indicator function ${1}_{A}(x)$ of a set $A$ equals $1$ if $x\\in A$ and $0$ if $x\\notin A.$ This can be written using Iverson bracket notation as\n\n$$\n{1}_{A}(x)=1[x\\in A].\n$$\n\nHere, the Iverson bracket expression $1[5\\in B]$ has $x=5$ and $A=B.$ Therefore, converting back to set indicator notation, we have\n\n$$\n1[5\\in B]={1}_{B}(5).\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167330 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c89b52a5-e7a3-566a-9918-62a16e6087de" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186165190} {:answer/id #uuid "f55fc263-98d6-5acb-9fd4-97e311d62182" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186165191} {:answer/id #uuid "02da13f5-fbac-5188-9cb2-808df4049ce9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186165192} {:answer/id #uuid "2ce84c4e-ac4e-5985-bea6-5f1e1ad5ff0e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186165193} {:answer/id #uuid "30554f8d-ed15-55d9-9885-c078507c90c5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186165194}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186165194} :answer-field/id #uuid "1590187d-85cc-5ce7-9d74-f442d233c0fa" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186165189}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "431d0042-1670-53b6-9a71-1178cea2315a" :question/math-academy-id "q-95022" :question/problem "Consider the following matrices:\n\n$$\n{B}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 7 \\\\ -5 & 0\\end{bmatrix},\\,{B}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 3 \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{bmatrix},\\,A=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & -1 \\\\ 3 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nIf $B=\\{{B}_{1},{B}_{2}\\}$ is a basis of $\\text{Span}\\{{B}_{1},{B}_{2}\\},$ and $$\n\\displaystyle [A]_{B}=\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{bmatrix},\n$$ then ${x}_{1}+{x}_{2}=$" :question/worked-solution "We need to find ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}$ such that \n\n$$\n{x}_{1}{B}_{1}+{x}_{2}{B}_{2}=A.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the given information into the above equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 7 \\\\ -5 & 0\\end{bmatrix}+{x}_{2}\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 3 \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & -1 \\\\ 3 & 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ \\begin{bmatrix}2{x}_{1} & 7{x}_{1}+3{x}_{2} \\\\ -5{x}_{1}-{x}_{2} & 0\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & -1 \\\\ 3 & 0\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating the corresponding entries, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}2{x}_{1}=-2 \\\\ 7{x}_{1}+3{x}_{2}=-1 \\\\ -5{x}_{1}-{x}_{2}=3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nThe equations above give a linear system with the augmented matrix\n\n$$\nM=[\\begin{array}{cc|c}2 & 0 & -2 \\\\ 7 & 3 & -1 \\\\ -5 & -1 & 3\\end{array}].\n$$\n\nRow-reducing the matrix using Gaussian elimination, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M= & [\\begin{array}{cc|c}2 & 0 & -2 \\\\ 7 & 3 & -1 \\\\ -5 & -1 & 3\\end{array}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+\\left(-\\frac{7}{2}\\right){R}_{1} \\\\ {R}_{3}:={R}_{3}+\\frac{5}{2}{R}_{1}\\end{aligned} \\\\ ∼ & [\\begin{array}{cc|c}2 & 0 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 3 & 6 \\\\ 0 & -1 & -2\\end{array}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe system is now \n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}2{x}_{1}=-2 \\\\ 3{x}_{2}=6 \\\\ -{x}_{2}=-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the second and third equations, we get ${x}_{2}=2.$ From the first equation, we get ${x}_{1}=-1.$ \n\nTherefore, $$\n\\displaystyle [A]_{B}=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nFinally, ${x}_{1}+{x}_{2}=-1+2=1.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167504 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "87432d69-96bb-52d4-838d-ce1f6c01f820" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186166233} {:answer/id #uuid "f17e4e3f-84c4-5252-b652-12737eea7c7d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186166234} {:answer/id #uuid "ff361c42-9dab-56e8-a7fa-2e6229a27e33" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186166235} {:answer/id #uuid "1e8d4f72-411d-5d3f-88dd-11650e216ee8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{6}{11}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186166236} {:answer/id #uuid "59e50954-5813-5a9b-9c99-a4c994779e1a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{11}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186166237}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166237} :answer-field/id #uuid "c48a4e74-626d-5c6c-a256-eb8d5fc6d57b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186166232}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "10558913-f96c-5656-8d0d-6962114123ec" :question/math-academy-id "q-97231" :question/problem "In a game show, a contestant can choose a prize from any box. The boxes are labeled with numbers $5$ through $15.$ If the contestant chooses a box at random, what is the probability that the box number they pick is less than $10?$" :question/worked-solution "Let $X$ represent the number on the chosen box. From the problem statement, we know that $X∼U\\{5,6,…,15\\},$ and we wish to compute $P(X<10).$\n\nIn general, if $X$ follows a discrete uniform distribution over a set $S,$ then $X$ has the following probability mass function:\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{|S|}, & x\\in S \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHere, we have $X∼U\\{5,6,…,15\\},$ and the set $S=\\{5,6,…,15\\}$ has $|S|=15-5+1=11$ items. So, we have\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{11}, & x=5,6,…,15 \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn $S,$ the items that satisfy $X<10$ are $5,6,7,8,9.$ There are $5$ of these items. Therefore,\n\n$$\nP(X<10)=5\\cdot \\frac{1}{11}=\\frac{5}{11}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167514 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0dc3d191-cba3-52f4-a11c-dde3db0a56cc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{14}{57}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186166293} {:answer/id #uuid "1af8addf-ed0d-5b82-873f-cf8c7cc8d138" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{31}{56}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186166294} {:answer/id #uuid "21102de4-1d55-5a18-bc2c-327303bd4608" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{29}{56}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186166295} {:answer/id #uuid "73dc79c3-61d5-5a44-b9d7-241c2f32a3dd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{15}{57}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186166296} {:answer/id #uuid "7b1fe4dc-1393-5f42-aead-cb877bdac66f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{15}{28}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186166297}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166297} :answer-field/id #uuid "2677ed19-d317-5fac-9ef2-ef42544595ee" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186166292}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "29f6e184-5ce2-54d0-991f-fb9cc535577d" :question/math-academy-id "q-97691" :question/problem "In a videogame, the number of obstacles that show up during a game can vary between $100$ and $155,$ inclusive. Assuming that each number of obstacles has the same probability to occur, what is the probability of having less than $130$ obstacles?" :question/worked-solution "Let $X$ represent the number of obstacles in a randomly selected game. From the problem statement, we know that $X∼U\\{100,101,…,155\\},$ and we wish to compute $P(X<130).$\n\nIn general, if $X$ follows a discrete uniform distribution over a set $S,$ then $X$ has the following probability mass function:\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{|S|}, & x\\in S \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHere, we have $X∼U\\{100,101,…,155\\},$ and the set $S=\\{100,101,…,155\\}$ has $|S|=155-100+1=56$ items. So, we have\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{56}, & x=100,101,…,155 \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn $S,$ the items that satisfy $X<130$ are $100,101,…,129.$ There are $30$ of these items. Therefore,\n\n$$\nP(X<130)=30\\cdot \\frac{1}{56}=\\frac{15}{28}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172612 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1eb6f98c-b45e-537c-a457-1f57056e9e85" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186519042} {:answer/id #uuid "7ae566ac-fe5f-5c58-a6f9-26a40344d78b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186519184} {:answer/id #uuid "90548aa5-959a-5095-a1ec-969585e26d47" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2} \\\\ \\frac{1}{2}\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186519230} {:answer/id #uuid "de84cd40-30b1-5bdf-bf92-d43dfaf0b310" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186519359} {:answer/id #uuid "e5262114-a0da-5731-a575-c6db872f7c47" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186519369}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186519184} :answer-field/id #uuid "567b4465-6b20-53a1-be8a-d9caef043ef1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186171297}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "22c685a9-35f7-5e7f-8909-4579d1ac4e02" :question/math-academy-id "q-93598" :question/problem "$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -3 \\\\ -3 & 1\\end{bmatrix},\\,D=\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -2\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nConsider the matrices shown above. \nGiven that $D={P}^{T}\\,AP,$ where $P$ is an orthogonal matrix, which of the following could be the first column of the matrix $P.$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that a matrix $A$ is orthogonally diagonalizable if there exists an orthogonal matrix $P$ such that\n\n$$\nA=PD{P}^{T},\n$$\n\nwhere $D$ is a diagonal matrix. Equivalently,\n\n$$\nD={P}^{T}AP.\n$$\n\nMoreover, a matrix $A$ is orthogonally diagonalizable if and only if it is symmetric.\n\nFirst, note that $A$ is symmetric and $D$ is diagonal. Therefore, $D={P}^{T}\\,AP$ gives an orthogonal diagonalization of $A.$\n\nFrom the diagonal matrix $D$ we deduce that the eigenvalues of the matrix $A$ are ${\\lambda }_{1}=4$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=-2.$ So, the first column of $P$ corresponds to the eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{1}=4.$ \n\nNow, we need to find an eigenvector that corresponds to ${\\lambda }_{1}=4.$ Computing $A-4I,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-4I & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -3 \\\\ -3 & 1\\end{bmatrix}-4\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-3 & -3 \\\\ -3 & -3\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have a system of linear equations with the augmented matrix $M$ which we reduce to row echelon form using Gaussian elimination, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{array}{cc|c}-3 & -3 & 0 \\\\ -3 & -3 & 0\\end{array}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-1){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{array}{cc|c}-3 & -3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{array}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix above has one pivot column (the $1$st one). Thus, ${x}_{2}$ is a free variable. From the first equation, we obtain ${x}_{1}=-{x}_{2}.$ Hence, the general solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}-{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{bmatrix},\\,{x}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nTherefore, setting ${x}_{2}=1,$ we get the eigenvector \n\n$$\n{v}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix},\n$$\n\nwhere $‖{v}_{1}‖=\\sqrt{2}.$ Dividing ${v}_{1}$ by its norm, we obtain the first column of $P:$ \n\n$$\n\\frac{{v}_{1}}{‖{v}_{1}‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\end{bmatrix}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172619 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "889010c9-939b-55f9-ad7c-496b3d4079bc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.0907" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186171340} {:answer/id #uuid "fdb5ea4f-37db-54ab-881e-8092d1c3598d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.0678" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186171341} {:answer/id #uuid "11366ddc-52e8-56d2-a3b8-d9dc670961da" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.0654" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186171342} {:answer/id #uuid "2ca07a65-6584-5597-bc1d-bc0eccef3a08" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.0471" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186171343} {:answer/id #uuid "7c8c99df-ca0e-53fa-8fd0-1710af52d767" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.0845" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186171344}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186171340} :answer-field/id #uuid "8cfdffc2-9fb3-5ca5-a79e-3de9c61edc4a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186171339}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "b7dad992-f274-5c2b-bce5-a1d7040d3be3" :question/math-academy-id "q-94948" :question/problem "At a particular underwater location, earthquakes occur continuously in time, independently, and at a constant average rate of once every $2.5$ months. Determine the probability that the next quake will not happen for at least $6$ months. Round your answer to four decimal places." :question/worked-solution "Since the earthquakes occur continuously in time, independently, and at a constant average rate, we can model the time until the next earthquake using an exponential distribution.\n\nAn exponential random variable $X$ with rate $\\lambda $ has the following probability density function:\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\lambda {e}^{-\\lambda x}, & x\\ge 0 \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet $X$ be the amount of time until the next earthquake. Since there is an earthquake every $2.5$ months on average, the rate is $\\lambda =\\frac{1}{2.5}=2/5$ earthquakes per month.\n\nThe number of earthquakes can be modeled as an exponential random variable with rate $\\lambda =2/5,$ and we can write \n\n$$\nX∼\\text{Exp}\\left(\\frac{2}{5}\\right).\n$$\n\nSo, $X$ has the following probability density function:\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{5}{e}^{-2x/5}, & x\\ge 0 \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the probability that the next earthquake will not occur for at least $6$ months is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X\\ge 6) & ={\\int }_{6}^{\\infty }f(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{6}^{\\infty }\\frac{2}{5}{e}^{-2x/5}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={-{e}^{-2x/5}|}_{6}^{\\infty } \\\\  & =-{e}^{-\\infty }+{e}^{-12/5} \\\\  & =0+{e}^{-12/5} \\\\  & ={e}^{-12/5} \\\\  & ≈0.0907.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172635 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a509034c-c45a-5da3-97a0-63e447121f98" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.3415" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186171436} {:answer/id #uuid "b56004d7-7f40-5724-8ddb-6040508abc14" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.2187" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186171437} {:answer/id #uuid "fb8b1bc6-5bf1-5707-a285-a177c1e41795" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.2652" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186171438} {:answer/id #uuid "2499ca89-587f-582f-b791-58c20a72926d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.3254" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186171439} {:answer/id #uuid "335d2d0e-05de-5522-a50e-86e00f91da1b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.4012" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186171440}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186171438} :answer-field/id #uuid "c1cb6741-c5bc-5c47-9b89-08e8784f4a69" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186171435}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "cb32904c-884e-5312-98bc-b88a7bbf47ea" :question/math-academy-id "q-96364" :question/problem "The total time until a computer component registers an error varies continuously in time, independently, and at a constant average rate of once every $30$ days. Determine the probability that a component will register its next error within the next $2$ to $12$ days. Round your answer to $4$ decimal places." :question/worked-solution "Since the errors occur continuously in time, independently, and at a constant average rate, we can model the time until the next error using an exponential distribution.\n\nAn exponential random variable $X$ with rate $\\lambda $ has the following probability density function:\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\lambda {e}^{-\\lambda x}, & x\\ge 0 \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet $X$ be the time until the next error. The time until the next error can be modeled as an exponential random variable. Since errors occur once every $30$ days on average, the rate is $\\lambda =1/30,$ and we can write \n\n$$\nX∼\\text{Exp}\\left(\\frac{1}{30}\\right).\n$$\n\n In this case, $X$ has the following probability density function:\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{30}{e}^{-x/30}, & x\\ge 0 \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the probability that first error will be registered between $2$ and $12$ days is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(2<X<12) & ={\\int }_{2}^{12}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{2}^{12}\\frac{1}{30}{e}^{-x/30}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-{e}^{-x/30}{|}_{2}^{12} \\\\  & =-{e}^{-2/5}+{e}^{-1/15} \\\\  & ≈0.2652\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172637 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "cb7dd9eb-cdeb-501a-a394-258b4c0a70a9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.6065" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186171448} {:answer/id #uuid "1b166edc-f9ee-54cf-a9dd-16b3169ff4a8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.0111" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186171449} {:answer/id #uuid "1e94cdee-8432-5001-bc67-ae18a90f7237" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.0498" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186171450} {:answer/id #uuid "268664a8-6e49-52a3-8c87-9405b615b645" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.2231" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186171451} {:answer/id #uuid "339dc04c-c725-5bea-bd34-5df67317f404" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.7165" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186171452}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186171450} :answer-field/id #uuid "02f76e3c-1245-5939-a1bc-92a2d23477ff" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186171447}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "ec6131e7-de2b-527c-b4ba-ef95dae870b5" :question/math-academy-id "q-96381" :question/problem "At a call center, the time taken for phone calls to be answered varies continuously in time, independently, and at a constant average rate of $3$ calls every minute. What is the probability of taking more than $1$ minute to answer the next call? Round your answer to $4$ decimal places." :question/worked-solution "Since the time until the next call is answered occurs continuously in time, independently, and at a constant average rate, we can model the time until the next call is answered using an exponential distribution.\n\nAn exponential random variable $X$ with rate $\\lambda $ has the following probability density function:\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\lambda {e}^{-\\lambda x}, & x\\ge 0 \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet $X$ be the time, in minutes, between calls. Then, since there are $3$ calls per minute on average, the rate is $\\lambda =3$ calls per minute.\n\nThe amount of time before the next call can be modeled as an exponential random variable with rate $\\lambda =3,$ and we can write \n\n$$\nX∼\\text{Exp}(3).\n$$\n\nSo, $X$ has the following probability density function:\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}3{e}^{-3x}, & x\\ge 0 \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the probability that the next call will occur after $1$ minute or more is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X\\ge 1) & ={\\int }_{1}^{\\infty }f(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{\\infty }3{e}^{-3x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-{e}^{-3x}{|}_{1}^{\\infty } \\\\  & =-{e}^{-\\infty }+{e}^{-3} \\\\  & =0+{e}^{-3} \\\\  & ={e}^{-3} \\\\  & ≈0.0498.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176236 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "98af9690-daea-569d-abed-f2b8041c29c3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186173675} {:answer/id #uuid "d7c44f9f-f601-585b-b63f-a6f53b8330c1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186173676} {:answer/id #uuid "09224f52-6123-5fc7-b6ac-a47bd7554bbb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186173677} {:answer/id #uuid "2a34fe20-af70-54d2-becf-f899ac18e198" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186173678} {:answer/id #uuid "74b8e7e8-c892-588f-9deb-c47d12e5ea72" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186173679}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186173676} :answer-field/id #uuid "a618000a-a584-5081-a7b4-598211aac365" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186173674}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "de16bbe1-6780-5e4f-aaa1-cd3ca96edf11" :question/math-academy-id "q-109408" :question/problem "$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 2 & 3\\end{bmatrix},\\,J=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 3\\end{bmatrix},\\,P=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & a & ∗ \\\\ 2 & b & ∗ \\\\ 1 & c & ∗\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nConsider the matrices above. Given that $A=PJ{P}^{-1}$ is a Jordan canonical decomposition of $A,$ find the value of $a+b.$" :question/worked-solution "First, notice that the Jordan matrix $J$ may be written as a composition of Jordan blocks, as follows:\n\n$$\nJ={J}_{2}(1)\\,⊕\\,{J}_{1}(3)\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n- a single $2\\times 2$ Jordan block corresponding to $\\lambda =1,$ and\n\n- a single $1\\times 1$ block corresponding to $\\lambda =3.$\n\nTherefore, the columns of $P$ form the following chains: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{J}_{2}(1) & : & {v}_{2}\\overset{A-I\\,}{\\to }{v}_{1}\\overset{A-I\\,}{\\to }0 &  \\\\ {J}_{1}(3) & : & {v}_{3}\\overset{A-3I\\,}{\\to }0 & \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs a result, we obtain that \n\n- the first column of $P$ corresponds to the eigenvector $$\n\\displaystyle {v}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$ of the eigenvalue $\\lambda =1,$\n\n- the second column of $P$ corresponds to the vector ${v}_{2}$ such that $(A-I){v}_{2}={v}_{1}.$\n\nNow, computing $A-I,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-I & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 2 & 3\\end{bmatrix}-\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -1 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 2 & 2\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the matrix equation $(A-I){v}_{2}={v}_{1}$ is equivalent to the system of linear equations with the augmented matrix $M$ which we reduce to row echelon form using Gaussian elimination, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{array}{ccc|c}1 & 1 & 0 & -2 \\\\ -1 & -1 & 0 & 2 \\\\ 3 & 2 & 2 & 1\\end{array}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+{R}_{1} \\\\ {R}_{3}:={R}_{3}+(-3){R}_{1}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{array}{ccc|c}1 & 1 & 0 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 2 & 7\\end{array}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{2}↔{R}_{3}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{array}{ccc|c}1 & 1 & 0 & -2 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 2 & 7 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{array}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix above has two pivot columns (the $1$st and $2$nd). Thus, ${x}_{3}$ is a free variable. From the second equation, we have ${x}_{2}=-7+2{x}_{3}.$ Substituting this into the first equation, we obtain\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}+(-7+2{x}_{3})=-2\\,⟹\\,{x}_{1}=5-2{x}_{3}.\n$$\n\nHence, the general solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}5-2{x}_{3} \\\\ -7+2{x}_{3} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{bmatrix},\\,{x}_{3}\\in R.\n$$\n\nTherefore, setting ${x}_{3}=0,$ we get  \n\n$$\n{v}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}a \\\\ b \\\\ c\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ -7 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nFinally, $a+b=5+(-7)=-2.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176240 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ccbed9d7-0c62-524b-bd6f-43f5bc5caf6b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186173699} {:answer/id #uuid "efa159ea-420f-5f96-bad7-8c208ac7bfa6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186173700} {:answer/id #uuid "fb75554f-a541-5e44-9da2-346a6de0a326" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186173701} {:answer/id #uuid "0b4edf31-b8dd-5ad6-b4cb-003fc8ba7923" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186173702} {:answer/id #uuid "6943d4e1-6ad1-5e69-b921-1f57f743a312" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186173703}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186173702} :answer-field/id #uuid "d543c614-8e9f-58e5-9583-bc03e394b2a6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186173698}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "32631d72-78ef-5ddd-8a62-4a6a56909f87" :question/math-academy-id "q-109417" :question/problem "$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 1 & -1 \\\\ -4 & 3 & 3\\end{bmatrix},\\,J=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix},\\,P=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & a & ∗ \\\\ -2 & b & ∗ \\\\ 1 & c & ∗\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nConsider the matrices above. Given that $A=PJ{P}^{-1}$ is a Jordan canonical decomposition of $A,$ find the value of $2a-b.$" :question/worked-solution "Since the Jordan matrix $J$ has a single $3\\times 3$ Jordan block corresponding to $\\lambda =1,$ the columns of $P$ form the following chain: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{v}_{3}\\overset{A-I\\,}{\\to }{v}_{2}\\overset{A-I\\,}{\\to }{v}_{1}\\overset{A-I\\,}{\\to }0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs a result, we obtain that \n\n- the first column of $P$ corresponds to the eigenvector $$\n\\displaystyle {v}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$ of the eigenvalue $\\lambda =1,$\n\n- the second column of $P$ corresponds to the vector ${v}_{2}$ such that $(A-I){v}_{2}={v}_{1}.$\n\nNow, computing $A-I,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-I & =\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 1 & -1 \\\\ -4 & 3 & 3\\end{bmatrix}-\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 0 & -1 \\\\ -4 & 3 & 2\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the matrix equation $(A-I){v}_{2}={v}_{1}$ is equivalent to the system of linear equations with the augmented matrix $M$ which we reduce to row echelon form using Gaussian elimination, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{array}{ccc|c}-2 & 1 & 0 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 0 & -1 & -2 \\\\ -4 & 3 & 2 & 1\\end{array}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{1}↔{R}_{2}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{array}{ccc|c}-1 & 0 & -1 & -2 \\\\ -2 & 1 & 0 & -1 \\\\ -4 & 3 & 2 & 1\\end{array}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+(-2){R}_{1} \\\\ {R}_{3}:={R}_{3}+(-4){R}_{1}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{array}{ccc|c}-1 & 0 & -1 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 2 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 3 & 6 & 9\\end{array}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{3}:={R}_{3}+(-3){R}_{1}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{array}{ccc|c}-1 & 0 & -1 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 2 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{array}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix above has two pivot columns (the $1$st and $2$nd). Thus, ${x}_{3}$ is a free variable. From the second equation, we have ${x}_{2}=3-2{x}_{3}.$ Substituting this into the first equation, we obtain\n\n$$\n-{x}_{1}-{x}_{3}=-2\\,⟹\\,{x}_{1}=2-{x}_{3}.\n$$\n\nHence, the general solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}2-{x}_{3} \\\\ 3-2{x}_{3} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{bmatrix},\\,{x}_{3}\\in R.\n$$\n\nTherefore, setting ${x}_{3}=0,$ we get  \n\n$$\n{v}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}a \\\\ b \\\\ c\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nFinally, $2a-b=2\\cdot 2-3=1.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176258 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0df9164a-2aee-5ef0-b081-85127f1a8451" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{f}_{Y}(y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{7}{y}^{2}, & 1\\le y\\le 2, \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186476226} {:answer/id #uuid "1c514f66-29f0-5f0b-bfe0-72c4620d0193" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{f}_{Y}(y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{4}{3}y, & 1\\le y\\le 2, \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186476264} {:answer/id #uuid "6d3e17cd-c3c8-537b-b290-5a1e7b10eed5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{f}_{Y}(y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{3}y, & 1\\le y\\le 2, \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186476446} {:answer/id #uuid "920faada-7711-5c7e-870a-65e8a1d7f033" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{f}_{Y}(y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{3}y, & 1\\le y\\le 2, \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186476516} {:answer/id #uuid "b2852486-2a3e-5a1c-8255-69aa2ae0c1fc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{f}_{Y}(y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}1, & 1\\le y\\le 2, \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186476604}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186476446} :answer-field/id #uuid "94e671b2-3bc6-5214-829c-28d39395095c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186173806}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "299fe748-fc78-5066-b383-fe1227671e5c" :question/math-academy-id "q-110409" :question/problem "Let $X$ and $Y$ be two continuous random variables with the joint probability density function\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{8}{3}{x}^{3}y, & 0\\le x\\le 1,\\,1\\le y\\le 2, \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFind the expression for the marginal PDF ${f}_{Y}(y).$" :question/worked-solution "If $X$ and $Y$ are two continuous random variables with joint probability density function $f(x,y),$ then the marginal density functions for $X$ and $Y$ are\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{X}(x)={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y,\\,{f}_{Y}(y)={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn our case, the marginal density function of $Y$ for $1\\le y\\le 2$ is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{Y}(y) & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{8}{3}{x}^{3}y\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{8}{3}y{[\\frac{1}{4}{x}^{4}]}_{x=0}^{x=1} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}y[{x}^{4}]_{x=0}^{x=1} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}y({1}^{4}-0) \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the full expression for ${f}_{Y}(y)$ is given by\n\n$$\n{f}_{Y}(y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{3}y, & 1\\le y\\le 2, \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176261 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "855080ee-7d77-5608-a727-98aeca63c799" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.896" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186173825} {:answer/id #uuid "d5c74169-51d2-5344-a44b-da35014e75ac" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.104" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186173826} {:answer/id #uuid "e9b25d28-14c6-5d5a-b7ed-d5edc59b963e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.658" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186173827} {:answer/id #uuid "515d0182-6f19-53ef-9998-81b80b6bb6e0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.205" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186173828} {:answer/id #uuid "7f7cb9cc-8702-5b2b-b64d-27610d91deb5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.342" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186173829}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186173825} :answer-field/id #uuid "8dd4eda8-fb5d-5a1b-b8c6-e12059aeaa19" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186173824}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "5dd8d5b2-3566-50a9-8091-3b7c245239df" :question/math-academy-id "q-110417" :question/problem "Let $X$ and $Y$ be two continuous random variables with the joint probability density function\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{2}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}), & 0\\le x\\le 1,\\,0\\le y\\le 1, \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFind $P(0.2<X<1).$" :question/worked-solution "If $X$ and $Y$ are two continuous random variables with joint probability density function $f(x,y),$ then the marginal density functions for $X$ and $Y$ are\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{X}(x)={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y,\\,{f}_{Y}(y)={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn our case, the marginal density function of $X$ for $0\\le x\\le 1$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{X}(x) & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{3}{2}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={[\\frac{3}{2}{x}^{2}y+\\frac{1}{2}{y}^{3}]}_{y=0}^{y=1} \\\\  & =[\\frac{3}{2}{x}^{2}+\\frac{1}{2}]-0 \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}{x}^{2}+\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the full expression for ${f}_{X}(x)$ is given by\n\n$$\n{f}_{X}(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{2}{x}^{2}+\\frac{1}{2}, & 0\\le x\\le 1, \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the required probability is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(0.2<X<1) & ={\\int }_{0.2}^{1}{f}_{X}(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0.2}^{1}(\\frac{3}{2}{x}^{2}+\\frac{1}{2})\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={[\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{3}+\\frac{1}{2}x]}_{0.2}^{1} \\\\  & =[\\frac{1}{2}(1)^{3}+\\frac{1}{2}(1)]-[\\frac{1}{2}(0.2)^{3}+\\frac{1}{2}(0.2)] \\\\  & =1-0.104 \\\\  & =0.896.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176262 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f5559f4b-1521-523b-8406-0bc5d66dde15" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186173833} {:answer/id #uuid "0256d74d-a236-57b6-be17-032aa4d685c6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186173834} {:answer/id #uuid "5d4e18a3-88e7-5f78-901b-41cc43b03702" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{x}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186506903} {:answer/id #uuid "8b6fd457-4eb9-5f91-a02c-38afa9b1bc0f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{x}^{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186506988} {:answer/id #uuid "eb17bacf-1aea-52d5-8089-fcb79fd9e5c4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2{x}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186507198}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186506903} :answer-field/id #uuid "d58894b2-ff7d-514e-beee-5c2f7fe8ec3c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186173830}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "d2b8ae16-dd41-57f5-a30f-634ebab68387" :question/math-academy-id "q-110422" :question/problem "Let $X$ and $Y$ be two continuous random variables with the joint probability density function\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}4xy, & 0\\le x\\le 1,\\,0\\le y\\le 1, \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe marginal CDF ${F}_{X}(x)$ of $X$ is given by\n\n$$\n{F}_{X}(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}0, & x<0 \\\\ g(x), & 0\\le x\\le 1 \\\\ 1 & x>1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWhat is the function $g(x)?$" :question/worked-solution "If $X$ and $Y$ are two continuous random variables with joint probability density function $f(x,y),$ then the marginal density functions for $X$ and $Y$ are\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{X}(x)={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y,\\,{f}_{Y}(y)={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn our case, the marginal density function of $X$ for $0\\le x\\le 1$ is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{X}(x) & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}4xy\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =4x{[\\frac{1}{2}{y}^{2}]}_{y=0}^{y=1} \\\\  & =2x[{y}^{2}]_{y=0}^{y=1} \\\\  & =2x({1}^{2}-0) \\\\  & =2x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the marginal CDF of $X$ for $0\\le x\\le 1$ is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{F}_{X}(x) & ={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{x}{f}_{X}(u)\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{x}2u\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & ={[{u}^{2}]}_{0}^{x} \\\\  & ={x}^{2}-0 \\\\  & ={x}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the full expression for ${F}_{X}(x)$ is\n\n$$\n{F}_{X}(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}0, & x<0 \\\\ {x}^{2}, & 0\\le x\\le 1 \\\\ 1 & x>1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, $g(x)={x}^{2}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176276 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "da9eb2d0-96bb-559d-b134-c96c4f67c9f6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{20}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186173915} {:answer/id #uuid "e7450f20-683f-5b29-9b91-ba327d9aa516" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{13}{200}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186173916} {:answer/id #uuid "0dbf191a-72d9-501b-82e3-2f6735f697ae" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{7}{200}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186173917} {:answer/id #uuid "2e457fee-1f1f-5f5d-a4f4-5fb241ee3b18" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{100}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186173918} {:answer/id #uuid "77d5ff7b-faf5-58c7-a6e7-ad993dda120c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{11}{100}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186173919}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186173917} :answer-field/id #uuid "c6870a21-257d-5058-aa1a-18345227a06c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186173914}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "2996061a-7fb9-5ed0-bf07-62fdec460468" :question/math-academy-id "q-111909" :question/problem "![](images/c6/c64cfa701997c2b9e5e54f7eb77afb9927b878065af9c32fed86f09f140a4ec4.png)\n\nLet $X$ and $Y$ be continuous random variables with the joint probability density function \n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}x+y, & (x,y)\\in D, \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf $F(x,y)$ is the joint CDF of $X$ and $Y,$ find $F(\\frac{1}{2},\\frac{1}{5}).$" :question/requires-calculator true :question/worked-solution "If $X$ and $Y$ are continuous random variables with the joint probability density function $f(x,y),$ then the corresponding joint CDF is given by\n\n$$\nF(x,y)=P(X\\le x,Y\\le y)={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{y}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{x}f(u,v)\\,\\text{d}u\\,\\text{d}v.\n$$\n\nLet's draw the point $(\\frac{1}{2},\\frac{1}{5})$ on our diagram. Let's also consider the region $Δ$ that lies below and to the left of this point.\n\n![](images/05/056fcbe25f83f488e1a88988d577535d0ef3776442f83e7d81560c6145f466a3.png)\n\nUsing the definition of the joint CDF, we have\n\n$$\nF(\\frac{1}{2},\\frac{1}{5})=P(X<\\frac{1}{2},Y<\\frac{1}{5})={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{1/5}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{1/2}f(u,v)\\,\\text{d}u\\,\\text{d}v.\n$$\n\nFor the purposes of computation, we're only interested in the region inside $Δ$ where $f(x,y)$ is nonzero. This is given by\n\n$$\nD\\cap Δ=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le \\frac{1}{2},\\,0\\le y\\le \\frac{1}{5}\\}.\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{1/5}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{1/2}f(u,v)\\,\\text{d}u\\,\\text{d}v & ={\\int }_{0}^{1/5}{\\int }_{0}^{1/2}(u+v)\\,\\text{d}u\\,\\text{d}v \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1/5}{[\\frac{{u}^{2}}{2}+uv]}_{u=0}^{u=1/2}\\,\\text{d}v \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1/5}(\\frac{1}{8}+\\frac{v}{2})\\text{d}v \\\\  & ={[\\frac{v}{8}+\\frac{{v}^{2}}{4}]}_{v=0}^{v=1/5} \\\\  & =[\\frac{1}{40}+\\frac{1}{100}]-0 \\\\  & =\\frac{7}{200}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176373 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a7af3a78-2189-55f7-924b-87ae9c355a8d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186174497} {:answer/id #uuid "b05955a7-09e3-533e-be48-b11c1351ed2e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186174498} {:answer/id #uuid "3255e560-bc47-508d-8647-48857909d6ab" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{27}{x}^{2}{y}^{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186523357} {:answer/id #uuid "747225ec-e6bd-529a-b8f8-19e90acd91dc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{27}{x}^{3}{y}^{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186523492} {:answer/id #uuid "d12e9958-4ff9-5e58-90db-4cd906dea6d2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{18}{x}^{3}{y}^{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186523694}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186174498} :answer-field/id #uuid "e8629fc7-417a-57b7-99e4-432922e8f5bc" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186174496}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "91c3afe4-eca4-53cf-a37b-d909dbee3477" :question/math-academy-id "q-143568" :question/problem "Let $X$ and $Y$ be continuous random variables with the joint probability density function \n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{3}{x}^{2}{y}^{2}, & 0\\le x\\le 1,\\,0\\le y\\le 3, \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFind the expression for the corresponding joint CDF when $x>1,\\,y>3.$" :question/worked-solution "If $X$ and $Y$ are continuous random variables with the joint probability density function $f(x,y),$ then the corresponding joint CDF is given by\n\n$$\nF(x,y)=P(X\\le x,Y\\le y)={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{y}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{x}f(u,v)\\,\\text{d}u\\,\\text{d}v.\n$$\n\nLet's draw the region $D$ in the $uv$-plane where the function $f(u,v)$ is nonzero. Let's also consider a point $(x,y)$ where $x>1,\\,y>3,$ and a region $Δ$ that lies below and to the left of this point.\n\n![](images/67/677dee4fb11cc609f089c8660ee16da5f9ac7572b27c9b0a3e97818e870b8db9.png)\n\nFor the purposes of computation, we're only interested in the region inside $Δ$ where $f(x,y)$ is nonzero. This is given by\n\n$$\nD\\cap Δ=D.\n$$\n\n \n\nSo our region $Δ$ covers the entire region where $f(x,y)$ is nonzero. Therefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{y}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{x}f(u,v)\\,\\text{d}u\\,\\text{d}v & ={∬}_{D}f(u,v)\\,\\text{d}u\\,\\text{d}v=1.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176374 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ccf84302-8540-5660-86b9-39475e7f3b1d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186174504} {:answer/id #uuid "3bf027e2-292e-5dd7-9aed-18cd61b400fe" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186174506} {:answer/id #uuid "906a169c-2edf-5f14-a67e-d3e7a3dc6d4d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{27}{x}^{3}{y}^{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186523557} {:answer/id #uuid "a291d4a2-1c32-59ce-a889-698e12ba88cd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{18}{x}^{3}{y}^{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186523596} {:answer/id #uuid "f2954d4e-2ef2-52c9-a341-a49445314421" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{27}{x}^{3}{y}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186523759}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186174504} :answer-field/id #uuid "3a226a41-ce63-5812-88a6-7616c15f80da" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186174502}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "09afba65-133d-5717-8265-02f123f360fe" :question/math-academy-id "q-143569" :question/problem "Let $X$ and $Y$ be continuous random variables with the joint probability density function \n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{3}{x}^{2}{y}^{2}, & 0\\le x\\le 1,\\,0\\le y\\le 3, \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFind the expression for the corresponding joint CDF when $x<0,\\,1<y<3.$" :question/worked-solution "If $X$ and $Y$ are continuous random variables with the joint probability density function $f(x,y),$ then the corresponding joint CDF is given by\n\n$$\nF(x,y)=P(X\\le x,Y\\le y)={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{y}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{x}f(u,v)\\,\\text{d}u\\,\\text{d}v.\n$$\n\nLet's draw the region $D$ in the $uv$-plane where the function $f(u,v)$ is nonzero. Let's also consider a point $(x,y)$ where $x<0,\\,1<y<3,$ and a region $Δ$ that lies below and to the left of this point.\n\n![](images/30/3035c8fb56868a761b7c57d0f86077b796fe573ac289726414bc5b75ea91ab58.png)\n\nFor the purposes of computation, we're only interested in the region inside $Δ$ where $f(x,y)$ is nonzero. This is given by\n\n$$\nD\\cap Δ=∅.\n$$\n\n \n\nSo there is nowhere in our region $Δ$ where $f(x,y)$ is nonzero. Therefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{y}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{x}f(u,v)\\,\\text{d}u\\,\\text{d}v & ={∬}_{Δ}f(u,v)\\,\\text{d}u\\,\\text{d}v=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176376 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b48da996-83e4-5e76-885d-d2f866efdb47" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{37}{10}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186174515} {:answer/id #uuid "beb1e493-b90f-56f2-8837-460b1c3b8a2e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{17}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186174516} {:answer/id #uuid "f94c187e-9eb8-5796-9ed1-fbaddd666f39" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{14}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186174517} {:answer/id #uuid "59462356-ec93-5845-9eb3-8534e160e05e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{29}{10}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186174518} {:answer/id #uuid "6380f064-7b18-5622-943f-749d9634d4bc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{27}{10}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186174519}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186174515} :answer-field/id #uuid "fe77e694-ed18-5bfe-9180-33e93463e7c0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186174514}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "2d805fb0-bc76-54a9-a263-7551f00436aa" :question/math-academy-id "q-143751" :question/problem "A random variable $X$ has the probability distribution given below.\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{10}, & x=0 \\\\ \\frac{1}{2}, & x=1 \\\\ \\frac{1}{5}, & x=2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nCalculate $\\text{E}[{X}^{4}].$" :question/requires-calculator true :question/worked-solution "If $X$ is a discrete random variable with probability mass function $f(x)$ and sample space $S,$ the expected value of ${X}^{4}$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\text{E}[{X}^{4}]={\\sum }_{x\\in S}{x}^{4}\\cdot f(x).\n$$\n\nSumming up the products of each squared value of $X$ and its associated probability, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[{X}^{4}] & ={\\sum }_{x\\in S}{x}^{4}\\cdot f(x) \\\\  & ={0}^{4}\\cdot \\frac{3}{10}+{1}^{4}\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}+{2}^{4}\\cdot \\frac{1}{5} \\\\  & =0+\\frac{1}{2}+\\frac{16}{5} \\\\  & =\\frac{37}{10}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186188535 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "82f79756-9008-5db9-a58e-f4dda6d20cda" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.75" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186185491} {:answer/id #uuid "87633653-db83-5aff-b7bd-240bf6394e52" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.375" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186185492} {:answer/id #uuid "d81e8b14-3433-54d9-8503-3e8daadbce31" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.25" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186185493} {:answer/id #uuid "3f0eea0e-e9ed-598c-a0ce-d2a51d1ad5ac" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.52" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186185494} {:answer/id #uuid "6f95668f-f5da-5049-abb0-85b1ac006401" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.125" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186185495}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186185493} :answer-field/id #uuid "186c6e8a-c524-5e91-8fb3-a11cea8703df" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186185490}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "0111779d-e047-5622-b502-1438439ba7f5" :question/math-academy-id "q-110390" :question/problem "Let $X$ and $Y$ be two independent continuous random variables whose marginal density functions are as follows:\n\n$$\n{f}_{X}(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}2x, & \\,0\\le x\\le 1, \\\\ 0, & \\,\\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\\,\\,{f}_{Y}(y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}3y, & \\,0\\le y\\le \\sqrt{\\frac{2}{3}}, \\\\ 0, & \\,\\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFind $P(X\\le 0.5,Y\\le \\sqrt{\\frac{2}{3}}).$" :question/requires-calculator true :question/worked-solution "Two continuous random variables $X$ and $Y$ with joint probability density function $f(x,y)$ are independent if and only if \n\n$$\nf(x,y)={f}_{X}(x)\\cdot {f}_{Y}(y)\n$$\n\n \n\nfor all possible values $x$ and $y,$ where ${f}_{X}(x)$ and ${f}_{Y}(y)$ are the marginal probability density functions for $X$ and $Y,$ respectively.\n\nSince our random variables are independent, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y) & ={f}_{X}(x)\\cdot {f}_{Y}(y) \\\\  & =2x\\cdot 3y \\\\  & =6xy,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $(x,y)\\in [0,1]\\times [1,\\sqrt{\\frac{2}{3}}].$ Therefore, the full expression for $f(x,y)$ is\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}6xy, & \\,0\\le x\\le 1,\\,0\\le y\\le \\sqrt{\\frac{2}{3}} \\\\ 0, & \\,\\text{otherwise.}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the required probability is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X\\le 0.5,Y\\le 1) & ={\\int }_{0}^{0.5}[{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{2/3}}6xy\\,\\text{d}y]\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{0.5}6x{[\\frac{{y}^{2}}{2}]}_{0}^{\\sqrt{2/3}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{0.5}2x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={[\\frac{2{x}^{2}}{2}]}_{0}^{0.5} \\\\  & =(0.5)^{2}-0 \\\\  & =0.25.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186188538 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b91c41cc-51b6-5e81-94b5-47e53eefedb5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "15" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186185509} {:answer/id #uuid "de2b3a92-bca2-5955-9f57-985d493d01c7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186185510} {:answer/id #uuid "1149e63f-1791-5d3e-9b6e-48e6553bdacc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "11" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186185511} {:answer/id #uuid "364e336f-8c62-54e4-a088-dac539c2c563" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186185512} {:answer/id #uuid "5f3f60e0-b192-58fc-95d2-6460f2bd7ca5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{7}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186185513}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186185511} :answer-field/id #uuid "e5b94b62-4b4a-5bbe-b08a-36b39f4d7d53" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186185508}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "24fd9d96-138b-563d-a252-06faa557f32d" :question/math-academy-id "q-110420" :question/problem "Let $X$ and $Y$ be two continuous random variables with the joint probability density function\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{2}{x}^{2}y, & 0\\le x\\le 1,\\,0\\le y\\le 2, \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nGiven that $\\text{E}[X]=\\frac{3}{4},$ find $\\text{E}[4X+6Y].$" :question/worked-solution "We're given $\\text{E}[X].$ So, we first need to compute $\\text{E}[Y].$\n\nThe marginal density function for $Y$ when $0\\le y\\le 2$ is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{Y}(y) & ={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{3}{2}{x}^{2}y\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}y[{x}^{3}]_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}y({1}^{3}-0) \\\\  & =\\frac{y}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the  expected value of $Y$ is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[Y] & ={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }y\\,{f}_{Y}(y)\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}y\\cdot \\frac{y}{2}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{2}{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{6}[{y}^{3}]_{0}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{6}(8-0) \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[4X+6Y] & =4\\,\\text{E}[X]+6\\,\\text{E}[Y] \\\\  & =4\\left(\\frac{3}{4}\\right)+6\\left(\\frac{4}{3}\\right) \\\\  & =3+8 \\\\  & =11.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186188546 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2972b8aa-250f-5bd2-8165-85701bb5ae9c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "Impossible to determine using only the given information." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186185557} {:answer/id #uuid "1b5b791a-0329-5126-9371-73ede1f87407" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f(x,y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}12{x}^{2}{y}^{3}, & \\,0\\le x\\le 1,\\,0\\le y\\le 1 \\\\ 0, & \\,\\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186477783} {:answer/id #uuid "8049d7d6-f564-5f57-bb96-831a621ad9c0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f(x,y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}4{x}^{2}{y}^{3}, & \\,0\\le x\\le 1,\\,0\\le y\\le 1 \\\\ 0, & \\,\\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186478002} {:answer/id #uuid "87ca1649-49ab-574f-a99b-ac6d040531af" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f(x,y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3{x}^{2}}{4{y}^{2}}, & \\,0\\le x\\le 1,\\,0\\le y\\le 1 \\\\ 0, & \\,\\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186478011} {:answer/id #uuid "fa496f92-afc6-5834-904b-3507c2afd467" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f(x,y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{4{y}^{2}}{3{x}^{2}}, & \\,0\\le x\\le 1,\\,0\\le y\\le 1 \\\\ 0, & \\,\\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186478232}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186185557} :answer-field/id #uuid "8245d5cc-a0d3-5155-a8be-2a54d7d8e0a9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186185556}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "b3e96d9d-5e09-5192-9419-9c5668ecfb87" :question/math-academy-id "q-136861" :question/problem "Let $X$ and $Y$ be two continuous random variables whose marginal density functions are as follows:\n\n$$\n{f}_{X}(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}3{x}^{2}, & \\,0\\le x\\le 1, \\\\ 0, & \\,\\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\\,\\,{f}_{Y}(y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}4{y}^{3}, & \\,0\\le y\\le 1 \\\\ 0, & \\,\\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFind the joint probability density function $f(x,y).$\n\n*Hint: We do not know if $X$ and $Y$ are independent.*" :question/worked-solution "Since we don't know whether $X$ and $Y$ are independent, it is impossible to determine the joint distribution of $X$ and $Y$ using only the given information."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186188547 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8fe78ca8-334b-531b-a512-a341ac492a5d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186185563} {:answer/id #uuid "c4be7868-6891-5064-82ca-d0abcd776c47" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186185564} {:answer/id #uuid "fcbd88c7-a64d-54bd-9593-2d1a47d427e4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186185565} {:answer/id #uuid "2262bddb-2ca0-514c-9db6-75b84a2dfe4f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186185566} {:answer/id #uuid "7afee5a8-f42b-5860-a3d2-673f5458e48c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186185567}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186185564} :answer-field/id #uuid "b9a62c9a-789e-53e7-a2b4-febcf8bd0baf" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186185562}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "e49ff8a6-6b90-594a-be32-eb49510ed619" :question/math-academy-id "q-136864" :question/problem "The joint probability density function for the continuous random variables  $X$ and $Y$ is given by\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{2}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}),\\, & 0\\le x\\le 1,\\,0\\le y\\le 1, \\\\ 0,\\, & \\text{otherwise.}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\nI. $$\n\\displaystyle {f}_{X}(x)=\\frac{3}{2}{x}^{2}+\\frac{1}{2}\n$$ for $x\\in [0,1]$\n\nII. $f(x,y)={f}_{X}(x)\\cdot {f}_{Y}(y)$ for all possible $x$ and $y$\n\nIII. $X$ and $Y$ are independent" :question/worked-solution "Two continuous random variables $X$ and $Y$ with a joint probability density function $f(x,y)$ are independent if and only if \n\n$$\nf(x,y)={f}_{X}(x)\\cdot {f}_{Y}(y)\n$$\n\n \n\nfor all possible values $x$ and $y,$ where ${f}_{X}(x)$ and ${f}_{Y}(y)$ are the marginal probability density functions for $X$ and $Y,$ respectively.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements.\n\n- Statement I is true. Computing the marginal density function for $X,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{X}(x) & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{3}{2}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}{[{x}^{2}y+\\frac{1}{3}{y}^{3}]}_{y=0}^{y=1} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}{x}^{2}+\\frac{1}{2},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \nwhere $x\\in [0,1].$\n\n- Statement II is false. Computing the marginal density function for $Y,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{Y}(y) & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{3}{2}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}{[\\frac{1}{3}{x}^{3}+x{y}^{2}]}_{x=0}^{x=1} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}{y}^{2}+\\frac{1}{2},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \nwhere $y\\in [0,1].$ So, when $x\\in [0,1]$ and $y\\in [0,1],$ we obtain that \n\n$$\n\\underbrace{\\frac{3}{2}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})}_{f(x,y)}\\ne \\underbrace{(\\frac{3}{2}{x}^{2}+\\frac{1}{2})}_{{f}_{X}(x)}\\cdot \\underbrace{(\\frac{3}{2}{y}^{2}+\\frac{1}{2})}_{{f}_{Y}(y)}.\n$$\n\n- Statement III is false. Since statement II is *not* true, our random variables $X$ and $Y$ are *not* independent.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186188561 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c9efbc4f-37b6-5dfc-a3af-79eea3983163" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.238" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186185647} {:answer/id #uuid "041e0497-134b-5de8-bc9b-1ea1cc88a289" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.345" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186185648} {:answer/id #uuid "16bccea1-d36b-5c0f-b068-3b702c40c2f4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.412" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186185649} {:answer/id #uuid "3c9f9576-00aa-5ad8-b83c-03c6b02f7706" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.253" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186185650} {:answer/id #uuid "79aa0e52-c2f0-5438-9e88-2676450da90c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.423" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186185651}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186185650} :answer-field/id #uuid "cf35cc72-3db3-5a32-afbf-f173e83fb7fa" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186185646}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "01f303ca-e9ff-5d45-9ae0-7430f5f2b5fe" :question/math-academy-id "q-143352" :question/problem "Let $X$ and $Y$ be two independent continuous random variables whose marginal density functions are as follows:\n\n$$\n{f}_{X}(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{2}\\sqrt{x}, & \\,0\\le x\\le 1, \\\\ 0, & \\,\\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\\,\\,{f}_{Y}(y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{3}y, & \\,1\\le y\\le 2, \\\\ 0, & \\,\\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFind $P(0\\le X\\le 0.4,Y\\le 2).$ Round your answer to three decimal places." :question/requires-calculator true :question/worked-solution "Two continuous random variables $X$ and $Y$ with joint probability density function $f(x,y)$ are independent if and only if \n\n$$\nf(x,y)={f}_{X}(x)\\cdot {f}_{Y}(y)\n$$\n\n \n\nfor all possible values $x$ and $y,$ where ${f}_{X}(x)$ and ${f}_{Y}(y)$ are the marginal probability density functions for $X$ and $Y,$ respectively.\n\nSince our random variables are independent, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y) & ={f}_{X}(x)\\cdot {f}_{Y}(y) \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}\\cdot \\frac{2}{3}y \\\\  & =y\\sqrt{x},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $(x,y)\\in [0,1]\\times [1,2].$ Therefore, the full expression for $f(x,y)$ is\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}y\\sqrt{x}, & \\,0\\le x\\le 1,\\,1\\le y\\le 2 \\\\ 0, & \\,\\text{otherwise.}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the required probability is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(0\\le X\\le 0.4,Y\\le 2) & ={\\int }_{0}^{0.4}[{\\int }_{1}^{2}y\\sqrt{x}\\,\\text{d}y]\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{0.4}\\sqrt{x}{[\\frac{{y}^{2}}{2}]}_{1}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{0.4}\\frac{3}{2}\\sqrt{x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={[{x}^{3/2}]}_{0}^{0.4} \\\\  & =(0.4)^{3/2}-0 \\\\  & ≈0.253.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186188563 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "95f313db-cd02-59f4-aa9c-cb2b1e94ddf4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186185659} {:answer/id #uuid "993dafd1-cd1f-5129-b778-e3d2650159e1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186185660} {:answer/id #uuid "fbed530d-6837-5d0b-b58e-2aa223dedb02" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186185661} {:answer/id #uuid "3512c16c-3729-51fc-a4e5-efb4b1b9d5aa" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186185662} {:answer/id #uuid "6dc005ec-f9c6-5f8a-a03a-55519f377e28" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186185663}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186185662} :answer-field/id #uuid "665dc965-8c73-559b-83c8-3ab6995229d7" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186185658}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "af8d6c3e-9622-5721-91e2-327a3d421c31" :question/math-academy-id "q-143384" :question/problem "The joint probability density function for the continuous random variables  $X$ and $Y$ is given by\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{3}x{y}^{3},\\, & 2\\le x\\le 4,\\,0\\le y\\le 1, \\\\ 0,\\, & \\text{otherwise.}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\nI. ${f}_{X}(x)=2x$ for $x\\in [2,4]$\n\nII. $f(x,y)={f}_{X}(x)\\cdot {f}_{Y}(y)$ for all possible $x$ and $y$\n\nIII. $X$ and $Y$ are independent" :question/worked-solution "Two continuous random variables $X$ and $Y$ with a joint probability density function $f(x,y)$ are independent if and only if \n\n$$\nf(x,y)={f}_{X}(x)\\cdot {f}_{Y}(y)\n$$\n\n \n\nfor all possible values $x$ and $y,$ where ${f}_{X}(x)$ and ${f}_{Y}(y)$ are the marginal probability density functions for $X$ and $Y,$ respectively.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements.\n\n- Statement I is false. Computing the marginal density function for $X,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{X}(x) & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{2}{3}x{y}^{3}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}x{[\\frac{{y}^{4}}{4}]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{6}x,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \nwhere $x\\in [2,4].$\n\n- Statement II is true. Computing the marginal density function for $Y,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{Y}(y) & ={\\int }_{2}^{4}\\frac{2}{3}x{y}^{3}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}{y}^{3}{[\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}]}_{2}^{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}{y}^{3}(8-2) \\\\  & =4{y}^{3},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \nwhere $y\\in [0,1].$ So, when $x\\in [2,4]$ and $y\\in [0,1],$ we obtain that \n\n$$\n\\underbrace{\\frac{2}{3}x{y}^{3}}_{f(x,y)}=\\underbrace{(\\frac{1}{6}x)}_{{f}_{X}(x)}\\cdot \\underbrace{(4{y}^{3})}_{{f}_{Y}(y)}.\n$$\n\n- Statement III is true. Since statement II is true, our random variables $X$ and $Y$ are independent.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186188722 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "af426a61-873a-54e8-87e7-c5268f358e01" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{20}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186186613} {:answer/id #uuid "d2a125c4-79a7-5fc7-8d69-81df40d1a785" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{10}{9}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186186614} {:answer/id #uuid "104fbeb9-321b-5cf2-a901-4e2171ef33e1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{20}{9}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186186615} {:answer/id #uuid "291d2635-4024-5090-9a86-02fd630f782c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{10}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186186616} {:answer/id #uuid "59ac25e1-139f-55c2-acdd-07c0ae67efdd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{9}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186186617}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186186615} :answer-field/id #uuid "3ff21e41-edd8-56fc-b9a8-ac593324b506" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186186612}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "ab2b344e-989b-53e3-aa40-0cb9cb3e7391" :question/math-academy-id "q-146773" :question/problem "$$\n{f}_{Y|X}(y\\,|\\,3)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{5}{2{y}^{7/2}}, & \\,1\\le y \\\\ 0, & \\,\\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet $X$ and $Y$ be continuous random variables. \nFind the conditional variance $\\text{Var}[Y\\,|\\,X=3]$ given that the conditional probability density function ${f}_{Y|X}(y\\,|\\,3)$ is shown above and the corresponding expected value is ${\\mu }_{Y|3}=\\frac{5}{3}.$" :question/requires-calculator true :question/worked-solution "Recall that the conditional variance of $Y$ given $X=x$ is defined by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Var}[Y\\,|\\,X=x] & =\\text{E}[(Y-{\\mu }_{Y|x})^{2}\\,|\\,X=x] \\\\  & =\\text{E}[{Y}^{2}\\,|\\,X=x]-{\\mu }_{Y|x}^{2},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere \n\n$$\n\\text{E}[{Y}^{2}\\,|\\,X=x]={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }{y}^{2}\\,{f}_{Y|X}(y\\,|\\,x)\\,\\text{d}y.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[{Y}^{2}\\,|\\,X=3] & ={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }{y}^{2}\\,{f}_{Y|X}(y\\,|\\,3)\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{\\infty }{y}^{2}\\cdot \\frac{5}{2{y}^{7/2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{\\infty }\\frac{5}{2{y}^{3/2}}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={[-5\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{y}}]}_{1}^{\\infty } \\\\  & =5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Var}[Y\\,|\\,X=3] & =\\text{E}[{Y}^{2}\\,|\\,X=3]-{\\mu }_{Y|3}^{2} \\\\  & =5-{\\left(\\frac{5}{3}\\right)}^{2} \\\\  & =5-\\frac{25}{9} \\\\  & =\\frac{20}{9}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186188724 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "820466ba-6f36-5d47-afef-0ee20288f099" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{10}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186186625} {:answer/id #uuid "a4fa1bcc-91b5-57e8-85ba-c02fe35e14e5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4}{9}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186186626} {:answer/id #uuid "cb641e71-4763-57b7-bdbf-54ac5c041138" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{9}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186186627} {:answer/id #uuid "f74163e9-f3d4-5fad-816e-1c659459e6c1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186186628} {:answer/id #uuid "08a51dff-0f49-52c1-ab38-9208cc65b517" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{34}{9}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186186629}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186186627} :answer-field/id #uuid "2338bb54-2402-5bab-b83e-832eb2b270cd" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186186624}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "3cefc73e-1ad2-58be-a1de-e39c1b293d17" :question/math-academy-id "q-146806" :question/problem "$$\n{f}_{X|Y}(x\\,|\\,-2)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{4}{{x}^{5}}, & \\,x\\ge 1 \\\\ 0, & \\,\\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet $X$ and $Y$ be continuous random variables. \nFind the conditional variance $\\text{Var}[X\\,|\\,Y=-2]$ given that the conditional probability density function ${f}_{X|Y}(x\\,|\\,-2)$ is shown above and the corresponding expected value is ${\\mu }_{X|-2}=\\frac{4}{3}.$" :question/requires-calculator true :question/worked-solution "Recall that the conditional variance of $X$ given $Y=y$ is defined by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Var}[X\\,|\\,Y=y] & =\\text{E}[(X-{\\mu }_{X|y})^{2}\\,|\\,Y=y] \\\\  & =\\text{E}[{X}^{2}\\,|\\,Y=y]-{\\mu }_{X|y}^{2},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere \n\n$$\n\\text{E}[{X}^{2}\\,|\\,Y=y]={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }{x}^{2}\\,{f}_{X|Y}(x\\,|\\,y)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[{X}^{2}\\,|\\,Y=-2] & ={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }{x}^{2}\\,{f}_{X|Y}(x\\,|\\,-2)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{\\infty }{x}^{2}\\cdot \\frac{4}{{x}^{5}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{\\infty }\\frac{4}{{x}^{3}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{\\infty }4{x}^{-3}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={[4\\cdot \\frac{{x}^{-2}}{(-2)}]}_{1}^{\\infty } \\\\  & ={[-\\frac{2}{{x}^{2}}]}_{1}^{\\infty } \\\\  & =0-(-2) \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Var}[X\\,|\\,Y=-2] & =\\text{E}[{X}^{2}\\,|\\,Y=-2]-{\\mu }_{X|-2}^{2} \\\\  & =2-{\\left(\\frac{4}{3}\\right)}^{2} \\\\  & =2-\\frac{16}{9} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{9}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186188793 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "01df44a6-657a-5670-a985-4a87b7716922" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{144}\\begin{bmatrix}7 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 11\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186547320} {:answer/id #uuid "0a71cefc-8de5-5de6-a1e7-e827ad772d4a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{144}\\begin{bmatrix}11 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 11\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186547337} {:answer/id #uuid "2529513a-9fa9-5a66-b66c-95b167283ef3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{144}\\begin{bmatrix}11 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 11\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186547386} {:answer/id #uuid "b1914b83-6794-52fe-91db-700c3ef7d55d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{144}\\begin{bmatrix}11 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 7\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186547703} {:answer/id #uuid "ef02e2b8-d181-5dc2-952d-5f459a779602" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{144}\\begin{bmatrix}11 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 7\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186547808}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186547337} :answer-field/id #uuid "f13c53ef-1e0a-55f0-86d4-e8d2d1425533" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186187038}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "150d719c-c9e6-5c71-9c3c-1c207b1e3bd9" :question/math-academy-id "q-148189" :question/problem "The random variables $X$ and $Y$ have the joint probability density function\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}x+y,\\, & 0\\le x\\le 1,\\,\\,0\\le y\\le 1, \\\\ 0,\\, & \\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nGiven that $\\text{E}[X]=\\text{E}[Y]=\\frac{7}{12},$ and $E[{X}^{2}]=\\frac{5}{12},$ find the covariance matrix for $X$ and $Y.$" :question/requires-calculator true :question/worked-solution "For two random variables $X$ and $Y,$ the covariance matrix $Σ$ is given by\n\n$$\nΣ=\\begin{bmatrix}\\text{Var}[X] & \\text{Cov}[X,Y] \\\\ \\text{Cov}[Y,X] & \\text{Var}[Y]\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nRecall that the variance of a random variable $X$ is determined by the formula\n\n$$\n\\text{Var}[X]=\\text{E}[{X}^{2}]-\\text{E}[X]^{2},\n$$\n\nand the covariance of two random variables $X$ and $Y$ is given by the formula\n\n$$\n\\text{Cov}[X,Y]=\\text{E}[XY]-\\text{E}[X]\\cdot \\text{E}[Y].\n$$\n\nFirst, we find $\\text{Var}[X].$ Using the given results, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Var}[X] & =\\text{E}[{X}^{2}]-\\text{E}[X]^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{12}-{\\left(\\frac{7}{12}\\right)}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{11}{144}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSimilarly, we compute $\\text{E}[{Y}^{2}]$ using the rule of the lazy statistician:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[{Y}^{2}] & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{y}^{2}\\cdot f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{y}^{2}(x+y)\\,\\text{d}x\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{1}(x{y}^{2}+{y}^{3})\\text{d}x\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{[\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{2}{y}^{2}+x{y}^{3}]}_{x=0}^{x=1}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(\\frac{1}{2}{y}^{2}+{y}^{3})\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={[\\frac{1}{6}{y}^{3}+\\frac{1}{4}{y}^{4}]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{6}+\\frac{1}{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{12}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Var}[Y] & =\\text{E}[{Y}^{2}]-\\text{E}[Y]^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{12}-{\\left(\\frac{7}{12}\\right)}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{11}{144}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, let's find $\\text{Cov}[X,Y].$ By the rule of the lazy statistician, we find $\\text{E}[XY]:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[XY] & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{1}xy\\cdot f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{1}xy(x+y)\\,\\text{d}x\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{1}({x}^{2}y+x{y}^{2})\\text{d}x\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{[\\frac{1}{3}{x}^{3}y+\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{2}{y}^{2}]}_{0}^{1}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(\\frac{1}{3}y+\\frac{1}{2}{y}^{2})\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={[\\frac{1}{6}{y}^{2}+\\frac{1}{6}{y}^{3}]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{6}+\\frac{1}{6} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, using the covariance formula, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Cov}[X,Y] & =\\text{E}[XY]-\\text{E}[X]\\cdot \\text{E}[Y] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}-\\left(\\frac{7}{12}\\right)\\cdot \\left(\\frac{7}{12}\\right) \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{144}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the covariance matrix is given by\n\n$$\nΣ=\\begin{bmatrix}\\text{Var}[X] & \\text{Cov}[X,Y] \\\\ \\text{Cov}[Y,X] & \\text{Var}[Y]\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{11}{144} & -\\frac{1}{144} \\\\ -\\frac{1}{144} & \\frac{11}{144}\\end{bmatrix}=\\frac{1}{144}\\begin{bmatrix}11 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 11\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186188845 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5ded94d4-f6db-5cd8-9fbd-892a2aec9b10" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1-\\frac{2}{{x}^{2}}-\\frac{2}{y}+\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}y}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186526693} {:answer/id #uuid "818e2fbb-826c-599b-9c21-644aa5a7adec" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1+\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}{y}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186526762} {:answer/id #uuid "a0b757df-503d-509d-9bb1-a13dcd28aa28" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1-\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}-\\frac{1}{y}+\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}y}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186526814} {:answer/id #uuid "ba331054-dad2-50ac-98dd-c8c0997ac923" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1-\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}-\\frac{1}{y}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186526858} {:answer/id #uuid "eee91643-fab4-507a-be86-e3d0341b84c2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1-\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}y}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186526975}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186526814} :answer-field/id #uuid "b432ba9e-dcac-5433-8ff8-190ee8bb1ea7" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186187350}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "18e3d97c-1951-59bd-b3e7-0c892c88f04a" :question/math-academy-id "q-159316" :question/problem "The marginal CDFs of two continuous independent random variables $X$ and $Y$ are given below. \n\n$$\n{F}_{X}(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}1-\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}, & x\\ge 1 \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\\,\\,{F}_{Y}(y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}1-\\frac{1}{y}, & y\\ge 1 \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFind the expression for the joint CDF when $x\\ge 1$ and $y\\ge 1.$" :question/worked-solution "If $X$ and $Y$ are independent continuous random variables with marginal CDFs ${F}_{X}(x)$ and ${F}_{Y}(y),$ respectively, then the corresponding joint CDF of $F(x,y)$ is given by\n\n$$\nF(x,y)={F}_{X}(x)\\cdot {F}_{Y}(y).\n$$\n\nTherefore, for $x\\ge 1$ and $y\\ge 1,$ the joint CDF for our random variables is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(x,y) & ={F}_{X}(x)\\cdot {F}_{Y}(y) \\\\  & =(1-\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}})(1-\\frac{1}{y}) \\\\  & =1-\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}-\\frac{1}{y}+\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}y}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the full expression for the joint CDF is\n\n$$\nF(x,y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}1-\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}-\\frac{1}{y}+\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}y}, & x\\ge 1,\\,y\\ge 1 \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186193073 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8e870738-b0c3-5a45-a958-0a8ab816e8ac" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186190325} {:answer/id #uuid "a6105e5f-10e9-560d-b7e2-58cef8643cfd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186190326} {:answer/id #uuid "c7b9be8a-5521-5f98-8ff2-7e30822d0744" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186190327} {:answer/id #uuid "fa218f47-8a14-524f-b582-ac051f2f4b5e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186190328} {:answer/id #uuid "6095d4ff-1396-56a8-a23b-76ac5552bb6d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{14}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186190329}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186190327} :answer-field/id #uuid "dbb35035-e4ad-5dfe-8305-ac38b8d7b733" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186190324}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "b377af59-2f66-52f6-af8e-4fb410b37e92" :question/math-academy-id "q-149715" :question/problem "Given that $X$ is a continuous random variable with the following probability density function, compute $\\text{E}[5X-2].$\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}1-{x}^{3}, & 0<x\\le 1, \\\\ \\frac{1}{8}, & 1\\le x<3.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, we simplify using the properties of the expected value:\n\n$$\n\\text{E}[5X-2]=5\\text{E}[X]-2\n$$\n\nFor a continuous random variable $X$ with probability density function $f(x)$ defined over a set $S,$ we can calculate the expected value using the following formula:\n\n$$\n\\text{E}[X]={\\int }_{S}x\\cdot f(x)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nSo, we calculate the expected value of $X$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[X] & ={\\int }_{0}^{3}x\\cdot f(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}x\\cdot (1-{x}^{3})\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{1}^{3}x\\cdot \\frac{1}{8}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(x-{x}^{4})\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{1}^{3}\\frac{x}{8}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={(\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}-\\frac{{x}^{5}}{5})|}_{0}^{1}+{\\frac{{x}^{2}}{16}|}_{1}^{3} \\\\  & =(\\frac{1}{2}-\\frac{1}{5})-0+(\\frac{9}{16}-\\frac{1}{16})-0 \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{10}+\\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[5X-2] & =5\\text{E}[X]-2 \\\\  & =5\\cdot \\frac{4}{5}-2 \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186199015 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3b8e61fe-84f0-5e8c-b8d0-debe0a505a86" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(2,4,-2)$ and $(3,6,-1)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186527428} {:answer/id #uuid "596f5dd6-00ad-5cc4-b4f1-c803276b16e5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(1,2,-3)$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186527479} {:answer/id #uuid "67eb7249-a648-56ce-b5ed-2207421c0d4e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(-2,-4,-6)$ and $(1,2,-3)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186527503} {:answer/id #uuid "6867a5b5-5ca3-5049-85eb-b68832f41cd2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(0,0,-4)$  only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186527507} {:answer/id #uuid "aa24d1c5-c858-5867-b4c5-389cc27692f6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(-1,-2,-5)$ and $(1,2,-3)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186527657}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186527503} :answer-field/id #uuid "f46b58ea-05c7-5cb0-b42c-c7ee3b8ec141" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186197960}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "e246b31d-b881-54d7-8871-a92812516505" :question/math-academy-id "q-94774" :question/problem "Find the points of the intersection of the elliptic cone ${x}^{2}+2{y}^{2}={z}^{2}$ and the line $x=\\frac{y}{2}=z+4.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we write the equation of our line in the parametric form:\n\n$$\nx=\\frac{y}{2}=z+4=t\\,⟹\\,\\{\\begin{aligned}x=t \\\\ y=2t \\\\ z=t-4,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).$\n\nSubstituting the above expressions for $x,$ $y,$ and $z$ into the equation of the elliptic cone, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+2{y}^{2} & ={z}^{2} \\\\ {t}^{2}+2(2t)^{2} & =(t-4)^{2} \\\\ {t}^{2}+8{t}^{2} & ={t}^{2}-8t+16 \\\\ 8{t}^{2}+8t-16 & =0 \\\\ 8({t}^{2}+t-2) & =0 \\\\ 8(t+2)(t-1) & =0 \\\\ t & =-2,1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we substitute our solutions back into the equations of the line.\n\n- For $t=-2,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}x=-2 \\\\ y=2(-2)=-4 \\\\ z=-2-4=-6,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nso the first point is $(-2,-4,-6).$\n\n- For $t=1,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}x=1 \\\\ y=2(1)=2 \\\\ z=1-4=-3,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nso the second point is $(1,2,-3).$\n\nTherefore, the points of intersection are $(-2,-4,-6)$ and $(1,2,-3).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186216959 :question/id #uuid "44ed3aff-8a4b-520c-808f-9e3ff928ac74" :question/math-academy-id "e-19814" :question/problem "The parabola $y = x^2 + 2$ and the line $y = 3x$ intersect at the points $(x_1, y_1)$ and $(x_2, y_2),$ where $x_1 <        x_2.$ What is the value of $x_1 + x_2?$" :question/worked-solution "We need to solve the following system consisting of the equations of the curves:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}y={x}^{2}+2 \\\\ y=3x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSetting the equations equal to one another and solving, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+2 & =3x \\\\ {x}^{2}-3x+2 & =0 \\\\ (x-1)(x-2) & =0 \\\\ x & =1,2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the $x$-coordinates of the intersection points are $x_1 = 1$ and $x_2 = 2.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\nx_1 + x_2 = 1 + 2 = 3.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186216961 :question/id #uuid "b24d4adc-8623-5fa9-9ac3-f95d7b4c0d1f" :question/math-academy-id "e-19816" :question/problem "The parabola $y=-x^2+6x$ and the line $y=9$ intersect at a single point with coordinates $(p,q).$ What is $p+q?$" :question/worked-solution "We need to solve the following system consisting of the equations of the curves:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}y=-{x}^{2}+6x \\\\ y=9\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSetting the equations equal to one another and solving, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-{x}^{2}+6x & =9 \\\\ -{x}^{2}+6x-9 & =0 \\\\ -({x}^{2}-6x+9) & =0 \\\\ -(x-3)(x-3) & =0 \\\\ x & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the $x$-coordinate of the intersection point is $x=3.$ \n\nWe know the $y$-coordinate of the intersection point from the equation of the line. So, the only point of intersection is $(3, 9).$ Therefore, $p=3,\\; q=9,$ and\n\n$$\np+q = 3+9 = 12.\n$$\n\nA sketch of the situation is shown below.\n\n![](images/f9/f9d09e07988752af26143fa2dc8df260784be5fcb16c28d8115bb44fc3c1a4a1.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186216962 :question/id #uuid "b2ead57b-8fd7-5d98-93fe-0e06290a5aa3" :question/math-academy-id "e-19817" :question/problem "Given that the line $y=x +2k$ does **not** intersect the parabola $y=x^2-x+3,$ what is the range of possible values of $k?$" :question/worked-solution "To determine whether the line $y=x +2k$ intersects the parabola $y=x^2-x+3,$ we need to solve the following system of equations for the two curves:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}y={x}^{2}-x+3 \\\\ y=x+2k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSetting the equations equal to one another and writing in standard form, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}-x+3 & =x+2k \\\\ {x}^{2}-2x+3-2k & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we solve this equation using the quadratic formula with $a=1,\\, b=-2,$ and $c=3-2k{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =\\frac{-(-2)\\pm \\sqrt{(-2)^{2}-4(1)(3-2k)}}{2(1)} \\\\  & =\\frac{2\\pm \\sqrt{4-12+8k}}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{2\\pm \\sqrt{8k-8}}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the line and parabola do not intersect, our quadratic equation must have no real solutions; hence, the expression inside the square root must be negative.\nSetting up and solving this inequality, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}8k-8 & <0 \\\\ 8k & <8 \\\\ k & <1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the range of possible values of $k$ is $k \\lt   1.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186221952 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8566dc89-42f7-5739-a0d1-347500de824f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/77/7705e0976e796f3a5642d68287dcfef5315c6599f7e997b56a34ec534b89e76d.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186220358} {:answer/id #uuid "a420fc82-2339-5078-a8e1-59c0d0d1479d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/bb/bb3187e3229b7fc09f530035d1dffe0e8690e8056502ecb27fcb41c274b6b218.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186220359} {:answer/id #uuid "b08617a8-44cd-5d28-8f4c-b9a7e2205dad" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/56/568be19a1553bf837519d82e11f8ee391c7dce120605fc2855039eeff83ff1fd.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186220360} {:answer/id #uuid "2a5769a2-ffdc-577f-8af7-36465be42670" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/af/af8a897bcc199d1f07332d6d8dd26cd7d6173943378777d72246909e89d7837b.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186220361} {:answer/id #uuid "5d388a75-c148-5fe9-8996-42f5a80dec0d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/e0/e0bf57ef5856cebd4b50666cb508039e26796d0f41fe4848cfa50e049f010c6f.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186220362}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186220361} :answer-field/id #uuid "6b61da0a-185b-51be-b559-d0e6be5de345" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186220357}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "48a05022-07e1-5409-ab9b-332660c73fa6" :question/math-academy-id "q-332874" :question/problem "$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)=x(t)-y(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)=x(t)-y(t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsider the system of linear differential equations above. The eigenvector corresponding to the only eigenvalue $\\lambda =0$ of the system's matrix $A$ is $v=[1,\\text{ }1]^{T}.$ The vector $w=[1,\\text{ }0]^{T}$ is such that $(A-\\lambda I)w=v.$  Which of the following could represent the phase portrait of the system?\n\n*Hint: Here, the dotted line represents the equilibrium solutions.*" :question/worked-solution "Since we have a repeated eigenvalue $\\lambda =0$ (of multiplicity two) and only one corresponding eigenvector $v,$ the solution of the system is given by\n\n$$\nx(t)={c}_{1}v{e}^{\\lambda t}+{c}_{2}(vt{e}^{\\lambda t}+w{e}^{\\lambda t}\\,),\n$$\n\nwhere $w$ is a solution to the equation $(A-\\lambda I)w=v.$\n\nIn our case, \n\n$$\n\\lambda =0,\\,v=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix},\\,w=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\n \nThus, the general solution of the system is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{bmatrix}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{bmatrix} & ={c}_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}+{c}_{2}(\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}t+\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}) \\\\  & ={c}_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}+{c}_{2}\\begin{bmatrix}t+1 \\\\ t\\end{bmatrix},\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFirst, notice that $$\n\\displaystyle {x}_{E}(t)={c}_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$ are equilibrium solutions of the system since ${x}_{E}^{′}(t)=0.$ These solutions form the line with the equation $y=x$ in the phase space, as shown below.\n\n![](images/30/309cbea2fa350c44c7a9225ec046b827113f9ffaa1dea6d746ab097e66cf945a.png)\n\nNow, we consider some general solutions of the form \n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{bmatrix}={c}_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}+{c}_{2}\\begin{bmatrix}t+1 \\\\ t\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nFor example:\n\n- When ${c}_{1}=1$ and ${c}_{2}=1,$ we get the following curve: \n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}x=2t+2 \\\\ y=2t+1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's add it to the phase space:\n\n![](images/d6/d6ec97d8fc3afffe41dca1dfd9d1918de586220408584183ef2eaad52e502485.png)\n\n- When ${c}_{1}=-1$ and ${c}_{2}=-1,$ we get the following curve: \n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}x=-2t-2 \\\\ y=-2t-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's add it to the phase space:\n\n![](images/09/0990ae7edeef543034fad206797305c41378b8243f809f5914f67c1e3b3955aa.png)\n\nThe phase portrait of our system looks as follows:\n\n![](images/af/af8a897bcc199d1f07332d6d8dd26cd7d6173943378777d72246909e89d7837b.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186221953 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0cea3552-0e9d-56f2-9e24-8d9c17e4c552" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$\\begin{bmatrix}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186546274} {:answer/id #uuid "2cf94921-2c23-5142-b223-bc58612d9c00" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}k \\\\ 2k\\end{bmatrix},\\,k\\in R" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186546346} {:answer/id #uuid "39ea73ba-1b0e-5076-8b87-cee93c2b2423" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$\\begin{bmatrix}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186546374} {:answer/id #uuid "7ba40f14-7f52-5d96-a933-5219eeaaffc4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}k \\\\ k\\end{bmatrix},\\,k\\in R" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186546525} {:answer/id #uuid "b17a8d12-de07-5930-9d74-4a3fab5e331c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}k \\\\ -k\\end{bmatrix},\\,k\\in R" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186546647}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186546647} :answer-field/id #uuid "a20c8207-9935-5207-8526-d46b30276bd3" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186220363}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "0e31d1b1-01e3-5911-a0b2-09271e054bd3" :question/math-academy-id "q-332876" :question/problem "$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)=x(t)+y(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)=2x(t)+2y(t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWhich of the following gives all equilibrium points of the system of linear differential equations above?" :question/worked-solution "Writing our system in matrix form, we have\n\n$$\nx(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 2\\end{bmatrix}x(t),\n$$\n\nwhere $$\n\\displaystyle x(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nThe equilibrium (fixed) points of a system ${x}^{′}(t)=f(x,y)$ are the points for which $f(x,y)=0.$ So, we get the following system of linear equations:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}x+y=0 \\\\ 2x+2y=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that the second equation is a multiple of the first one. So, we can disregard the second equation. \n\nFrom first equation, we get\n\n$$\nx+y=0\\,⇒\\,y=-x.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equilibrium points are given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}k \\\\ -k\\end{bmatrix},\\,k\\in R.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186222007 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2a827329-a0ff-550f-bb7e-ee23bd0d15dd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\{\\begin{aligned}{u}^{′}(t)={u}^{2}(t)+2u(t) \\\\ {v}^{′}(t)=-2u(t)-{v}^{2}(t)\\end{aligned}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186552947} {:answer/id #uuid "8bcfc10f-6abc-5282-81a9-6b42e5fe2cc5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\{\\begin{aligned}{u}^{′}(t)={u}^{2}(t)+{v}^{2}(t) \\\\ {v}^{′}(t)=-2u(t)+v(t)\\end{aligned}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186553153} {:answer/id #uuid "ac44864b-4825-549b-a5af-a7b6b9f978f4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\{\\begin{aligned}{u}^{′}(t)={u}^{2}(t)+2u(t)+v(t) \\\\ {v}^{′}(t)=-2u(t)+v(t)-{v}^{2}(t)\\end{aligned}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186553214} {:answer/id #uuid "c691a6d3-d876-51c8-b913-9e1a8fbf2d8a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\{\\begin{aligned}{u}^{′}(t)={u}^{2}(t)+2u(t)+{v}^{2}(t) \\\\ {v}^{′}(t)=u(t)-v(t)\\end{aligned}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186553263} {:answer/id #uuid "d0155f97-773b-5d90-9876-d81f9a834c19" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\{\\begin{aligned}{u}^{′}(t)=2{u}^{2}(t)+v(t) \\\\ {v}^{′}(t)=-u(t)-{v}^{2}(t)\\end{aligned}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186553285}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186553214} :answer-field/id #uuid "5cc81f40-30f6-5eea-8198-03adefd62b6b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186220716}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "65e63d99-f774-5779-ae22-f06fdcf67250" :question/math-academy-id "q-335373" :question/problem "$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)={x}^{2}(t)+2x(t)+y(t)-1 \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)=-2x(t)+3y(t)-{y}^{2}(t)-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsider the system of ODEs above. An equilibrium point of the system is $[0,\\,1]^{T}.$ Which of the following is the system obtained by shifting this equilibrium to the origin?" :question/worked-solution "To shift the system's equilibrium from ${x}^{∗}$ to the origin, we define the deviation vector\n\n$$\nu=x-{x}^{∗},\n$$\n\nwhere $$\n\\displaystyle x=\\begin{bmatrix}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{bmatrix}\n$$ and $$\n\\displaystyle u=\\begin{bmatrix}u(t) \\\\ v(t)\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\nu=x-\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}\\,⇒\\,x=u+\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}\\,\\text{and}\\,{x}^{′}={u}^{′}.\n$$\n\nIn coordinate form, this can be written as follows:\n\n$$\nx(t)=u(t),\\,y(t)=v(t)+1,\\,{x}^{′}(t)={u}^{′}(t),\\,{v}^{′}(t)={y}^{′}(t)\n$$\n\nLet's now substitute these into the given system.\n\n- Substituting into the first equation, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t) & ={x}^{2}(t)+2x(t)+y(t)-1 \\\\ {u}^{′}(t) & ={u}^{2}(t)+2u(t)+(v(t)+1)-1 \\\\  & ={u}^{2}(t)+2u(t)+v(t).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Substituting into the second equation, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}(t) & =-2x(t)+3y(t)-{y}^{2}(t)-2 \\\\ {v}^{′}(t) & =-2u(t)+3(v(t)+1)-(v(t)+1)^{2}-2 \\\\  & =-2u(t)+3v(t)+3-({v}^{2}(t)+2v(t)+1)-2 \\\\  & =-2u(t)+v(t)-{v}^{2}(t).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the shifted system is the following:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{u}^{′}(t)={u}^{2}(t)+2u(t)+v(t) \\\\ {v}^{′}(t)=-2u(t)+v(t)-{v}^{2}(t)\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186222024 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "00e95e33-347b-57a5-99b1-b6350c6a1b82" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186311966}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186311966} :answer-field/id #uuid "7b992e7c-efa0-59cc-a0fb-844e1ecd2b0b" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186314338}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "0ed7ffbf-06f9-50a7-bc59-05cb4e317a52" :question/math-academy-id "q-340873" :question/problem "$$\nf(x)∼\\frac{{a}_{0}}{2}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi }{2}x)+{b}_{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi }{2}x)\n$$\n\nThe Fourier series of the $4$-periodic extension $f(x)$ of the function \n\n$$\nF(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}2, & x\\in [0,2) \\\\ -1, & x\\in [-2,0)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nis shown above. Find the exact value of ${a}_{3}.$\n\n${a}_{3}=$ {{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "For the Fourier series of a $2L$-periodic function, the cosine coefficients are given by\n\n$$\n{a}_{n}=\\frac{1}{L}{\\int }_{-L}^{L}f(x)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi }{L}x)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nIn our case, $L=2.$ Also, $f(x)=2$ for $x\\in (0,2)$ and $f(x)=-1$ for $x\\in (-2,0).$ So, substituting into the formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{3} & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{-2}^{2}f(x)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{3\\pi }{2}x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{-2}^{0}(-1)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{3\\pi }{2}x)\\,\\text{d}x+\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{2}2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{3\\pi }{2}x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot \\frac{2}{3\\pi }{[\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{3\\pi }{2}x)]}_{-2}^{0}+\\frac{2}{3\\pi }{[\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{3\\pi }{2}x)]}_{0}^{2} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{3\\pi }(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(0)-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(-3\\pi ))+\\frac{2}{3\\pi }(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3\\pi )-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(0)) \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186222026 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9c1dc147-dc00-572f-8894-ac569274ec86" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "False" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186220831} {:answer/id #uuid "d57a9bf1-9eb8-5a62-af93-f0adccd2c02a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "True" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186220832}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186220831} :answer-field/id #uuid "7dc31a51-5a22-52a2-82a9-b8bfbc4a6c09" :answer-field/key "field-4" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186220830} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a439efcd-2a10-585c-b283-559ad9cc5042" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "False" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186220834} {:answer/id #uuid "04d5be8a-ccd5-5ba0-af8a-6bb955d973d2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "True" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186220835}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186220834} :answer-field/id #uuid "245f6509-819f-5ccd-aa42-039b0427e209" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186220833} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "acedd8cc-9d61-52b1-8999-d4be3a37283c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "True" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186220837} {:answer/id #uuid "7f2ceeb0-5d68-5fcd-a112-aa399d54ce2c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "False" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186220838}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186220838} :answer-field/id #uuid "d52bb1d1-924a-5136-870b-26d714a6575b" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186220836} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d4f4be6d-2315-5939-860c-03d41e63b3e8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "False" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186220840} {:answer/id #uuid "61611f6b-b265-55e4-bc14-70414548702e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "True" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186220841}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186220841} :answer-field/id #uuid "eb4aaf3f-0c92-59b9-9f4d-a2ff09846c50" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186220839}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "236f9cfb-2280-5b96-a723-95c1ab1f82f6" :question/math-academy-id "q-341153" :question/problem "$$\nF(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}1, & x\\in [0,1) \\\\ x, & x\\in [-1,0)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe above function $F(x)$ is defined on $x\\in [-1,1).$ \nSelect the correct options below.\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"right\" data-border-top=\"none\" data-border-right=\"none\" data-border-bottom=\"none\" data-border-left=\"none\">I.</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"left\" data-border-top=\"none\" data-border-right=\"none\" data-border-bottom=\"none\" data-border-left=\"none\">$F(x)$ is continuous on $x\\in (-1,1)$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-border-top=\"none\" data-border-right=\"none\" data-border-bottom=\"none\" data-border-left=\"none\">{{field-1}}</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"right\" data-border-top=\"none\" data-border-right=\"none\" data-border-bottom=\"none\" data-border-left=\"none\">II.</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"left\" data-border-top=\"none\" data-border-right=\"none\" data-border-bottom=\"none\" data-border-left=\"none\">$F(x)$ is piecewise smooth on $x\\in (-1,1)$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-border-top=\"none\" data-border-right=\"none\" data-border-bottom=\"none\" data-border-left=\"none\">{{field-2}}</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"right\" data-border-top=\"none\" data-border-right=\"none\" data-border-bottom=\"none\" data-border-left=\"none\">III.</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"left\" data-border-top=\"none\" data-border-right=\"none\" data-border-bottom=\"none\" data-border-left=\"none\">$\\lim_{x\\to -{1}^{+}}F(x)=\\lim_{x\\to {1}^{-}}F(x)$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-border-top=\"none\" data-border-right=\"none\" data-border-bottom=\"none\" data-border-left=\"none\">{{field-3}}</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"right\" data-border-top=\"none\" data-border-right=\"none\" data-border-bottom=\"none\" data-border-left=\"none\">IV.</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"left\" data-border-top=\"none\" data-border-right=\"none\" data-border-bottom=\"none\" data-border-left=\"none\">$f(x)$ is piecewise smooth and continuous on $R$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-border-top=\"none\" data-border-right=\"none\" data-border-bottom=\"none\" data-border-left=\"none\">{{field-4}}</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n" :question/worked-solution "Let's examine the given statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is $\\text{false}.$ The function $F(x)$ has a jump discontinuity at $x=0.$ Its graph is shown below.\n\n![](images/2e/2eb2120db83d33d37f1d076f7e3664a21cd1bb97ccd04aeb792cd8e510edf846.png)\n\n- Statement II is $\\text{true}.$ The function $F(x)$ is piecewise smooth on $x\\in (-1,1).$ It consists of two smooth pieces.\n\n- Statement III is $\\text{false}.$ Indeed, we have\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to -{1}^{+}}F(x)=\\lim_{x\\to -{1}^{+}}x=-1\n$$\n\nand \n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to {1}^{-}}F(x)=\\lim_{x\\to {1}^{-}}1=1.\n$$\n\nSo, $\\lim_{x\\to -{1}^{+}}F(x)\\ne {lim}_{x\\to {1}^{-}}F(x).$\n\n- Statement IV is $\\text{false}$ since statements I and III are false.\n\n**Note:** The $2$-periodic extension of $F(x)$ to $x\\in R$ has the graph shown below.\n\n![](images/c9/c91cf3eea948fdce9a992c77d95bc3c8bc5165ade9e6f17ae5b7e699b0a9be9d.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186234005 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4b865f1f-3c5c-5985-bbf9-f3e42ab7b33b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "b=-1,\\,c=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321741} {:answer/id #uuid "71dd4a6f-34e4-573a-9c0e-b15bbfcb0163" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "b=1,\\,c=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322043} {:answer/id #uuid "b04c19b3-7728-5118-989a-8a737acbfb86" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "b=1,\\,c=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322529} {:answer/id #uuid "be5e5c90-a85c-57cf-872f-efc3d280f16c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "b=1,\\,c=-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322627} {:answer/id #uuid "cec67ce0-7190-5ede-89a6-f11b2ab663be" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "b=-1,\\,c=-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322761}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186322761} :answer-field/id #uuid "bd5de254-6076-5d93-a6dc-dbe31586f3ee" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323538}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "31b0eebe-277e-54d7-a4c5-c64f7d79456e" :question/math-academy-id "q-36743" :question/problem "For the piecewise function \n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+2x+b\\, & \\text{ for }\\,x\\le 1 \\\\ {x}^{2}+cx+2\\, & \\text{ for }\\,x>1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nit is known that $\\lim_{x\\to 1}f(x)=2.$ What is the value of the constants $b$ and $c?$" :question/worked-solution "In order for $\\lim_{x\\to 1}f(x)$ to be equal to $2,$ the left and right-sided limits must both exist and be equal to $2.$\n\n- Approaching from the left, we have $x<1,$ so we use the expression $f(x)={x}^{2}+2x+b.$ We get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to {1}^{-}}f(x) & =\\lim_{x\\to {1}^{-}}({x}^{2}+2x+b) \\\\  & ={1}^{2}+2(1)+b \\\\  & =1+2+b \\\\  & =b+3\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the left-sided limit must be equal to $2,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to {1}^{-}}f(x) & =2 \\\\ b+3 & =2 \\\\ b & =2-3 \\\\ b & =-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Approaching from the right, we have $x>1,$ so we use the expression $f(x)={x}^{2}+cx+2.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to {1}^{+}}f(x) & =\\lim_{x\\to {1}^{+}}({x}^{2}+cx+2) \\\\  & ={1}^{2}+c(1)+2 \\\\  & =1+c+2 \\\\  & =c+3\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the right-sided limit must be equal to $2,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to {1}^{+}}f(x) & =2 \\\\ c+3 & =2 \\\\ c & =2-3 \\\\ c & =-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186234014 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "27c9870f-0506-5064-ac23-b292234d4b54" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186503057} {:answer/id #uuid "2dc73b7a-ef3d-547d-a954-d41ff011c35f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186503064} {:answer/id #uuid "34bb0305-64c5-5b32-bbd0-485d92cf9acc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186503076} {:answer/id #uuid "38b73058-6d97-5937-90c5-70fa98985408" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186503083} {:answer/id #uuid "f7fcce4d-e5c7-52f8-9d54-5a89dffa0fb6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186503489}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186503083} :answer-field/id #uuid "aa09ee35-3713-5a66-963f-92ef86cb648a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186503601}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "d3161c6d-15b1-5502-a086-55781c56e488" :question/math-academy-id "q-38592" :question/problem "Let $f(x)$ be the function defined below. For what value of $k$ is $f(x)$ continuous at $x=-1?$\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{k}{x-1}, & x<-1 \\\\ 2x-k, & x\\ge -1\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "From the definition of $f(x)$, the left-sided limit of $f(x)$ at $x=-1$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to -{1}^{-}}f(x) & =\\lim_{x\\to -{1}^{-}}\\frac{k}{x-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{k}{-1-1} \\\\  & =-\\frac{k}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nOn the other hand, the right-sided limit is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to -{1}^{+}}f(x) & =\\lim_{x\\to -{1}^{+}}(2x-k) \\\\  & =2(-1)-k \\\\  & =-2-k.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe also note that $f(-1)=-2-k.$ \n\nSo, for $f(x)$ to be continuous at $x=-1,$ we must have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to -{1}^{+}}f(x) & =\\lim_{x\\to -{1}^{-}}f(x) \\\\ -2-k & =-\\frac{k}{2} \\\\ -4-2k & =-k \\\\ k & =-4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $k=-4$ removes the discontinuity at $x=-1.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186234352 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "16834030-119c-50b4-9662-edd5f91fc424" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186503027} {:answer/id #uuid "565b2c1d-8909-5088-ae15-f6d4e0aa6c5f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186503149} {:answer/id #uuid "63422371-897b-542b-9d8a-828fa718a920" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186503173} {:answer/id #uuid "d4ef85bf-0c33-54a7-91ff-75cb78a92187" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186503404} {:answer/id #uuid "f24c9e76-cd98-5133-8f17-177d55d12f66" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186503473}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186503027} :answer-field/id #uuid "488d2faf-a1cd-5796-984d-787dccc3cd23" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186503542}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "bf7b7ef5-50f5-5f18-b878-7ff1f9484b60" :question/math-academy-id "q-73492" :question/problem "Suppose that $f(x)$ is defined as\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x, & x\\le 0 \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡x, & x>0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nOn which of the following intervals can the extreme value theorem be applied to the function?\n\nI. $[-2,-1]$\n\nII. $[-1,1]$\n\nIII. $(0,1]$" :question/worked-solution "Remember that the extreme value theorem is only applicable to closed intervals on which the function is continuous.\n\nSo, let's check whether the function is continuous on each of the intervals.\n\n- First, consider $[-2,-1].$ In this interval, $f(x)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡x$ and so $f(x)$ is continuous on $[-2,-1].$  Therefore, the extreme value theorem is applicable on this interval.\n\n- Now, consider the interval $[-1,1].$ In this case, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to {0}^{+}}f(x) & =\\lim_{x\\to {0}^{+}}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)=-\\infty .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, $f(x)$ has an essential discontinuity at $x=0.$ Therefore, $f(x)$ is not continuous on the interval $[-1,1],$ and the extreme value theorem is not applicable on this interval.\n\n- Finally, we note that the interval $(0,1]$ is not closed, so the extreme value theorem is not applicable.\n\nIn conclusion, the correct answer is \"I only\"."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186240801 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "50ff7ec6-76c4-5b74-bf1d-be84c817c24b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\lim_{x\\to {3}^{-}}f(x)>0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186238755} {:answer/id #uuid "54122348-0530-5cb3-b939-847df168d38e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$f(3)$  does not exist" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186238777} {:answer/id #uuid "a33bcdd9-91d5-5f8b-a44e-d43311963d22" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\lim_{x\\to {3}^{-}}f(x)\\ne 3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186239476} {:answer/id #uuid "c0cdaeed-395b-51a0-82a0-64a886e4efb2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\lim_{x\\to {3}^{-}}f(x)\\ne {lim}_{x\\to {3}^{+}}f(x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186239737} {:answer/id #uuid "dc1fa30c-32a7-5587-8fe4-d9afe2475fdd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\lim_{x\\to {3}^{-}}f(x)\\ne f(3)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186239975}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186238777} :answer-field/id #uuid "fb6443c8-739f-5411-886c-c1f2567fc824" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186240768}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "cb39799c-0b6e-5be1-b620-9cc6473bd49a" :question/math-academy-id "q-104129" :question/problem "For what reason does the function $f(x)$ given below have a discontinuity at $x=3?$\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{5}{x-3}, & x<3 \\\\ -\\frac{5}{x-3}, & x>3\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Let's check the conditions necessary for $f(x)$ to be continuous at $x=3.$\n\n- We have that $f(3)$ is not defined since $x=3$ does not belong to the domain of $f(x).$ $\\times $\n\nTherefore, the function $f(x)$ has a discontinuity at $x=3$ because $f(3)$ does not exist."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186240803 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "11b040a2-7645-58f8-9b41-03e61af92932" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$\\lim_{x\\to (-2)}f(x)$ does not exist" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186238203} {:answer/id #uuid "c63d1d7b-fe91-5f68-8507-be316b42d719" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$f(x)$ is a piecewise function" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186239799} {:answer/id #uuid "d99a8202-4c5f-58ae-b994-4605c367f59b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$f(-2)$ does not exist" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186239952} {:answer/id #uuid "ea6d06d9-c507-5f74-a51e-07af771eddee" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$\\lim_{x\\to (-2)^{+}}f(x)$ does not exist" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186240118} {:answer/id #uuid "eab4ba20-4e19-5aba-a102-ad9128d50961" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\lim_{x\\to (-2)}f(x)\\ne f(-2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186240120}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186240120} :answer-field/id #uuid "ca346b04-01ff-54ea-ad48-0975c9bdfa88" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186240684}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "65ba7f63-40be-5e71-8f5e-9f52e8de1734" :question/math-academy-id "q-104132" :question/problem "For what reason does the function $f(x)$ below have a discontinuity at $x=-2?$ \n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{x}^{2}+2}{2}, & x<-2 \\\\ 5, & x=-2 \\\\ \\frac{18}{{x}^{2}+2}, & x>-2\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Let's check the conditions necessary for $f(x)$ to be continuous at $x=-2.$\n\n- $f(-2)=5,$ so $f(-2)$ exists. $✓$\n\n- We calculate the one-sided limits and find that they coincide:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to (-2)^{-}}f(x) & =\\lim_{x\\to (-2)^{-}}\\left(\\frac{{x}^{2}+2}{2}\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{(-2)^{2}+2}{2} \\\\  & =3, \\\\ \\lim_{x\\to (-2)^{+}}f(x) & =\\lim_{x\\to (-2)^{+}}\\left(\\frac{18}{{x}^{2}+2}\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{18}{(-2)^{2}+2} \\\\  & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the overall limit exists and $\\lim_{x\\to (-2)}f(x)=3.\\,\\,✓$ $\\,\\,\\,$\n\n- The limit of the function is not equal to the function value: $\\lim_{x\\to (-2)}f(x)=3,$ while $f(-2)=5.$ $\\times $\n\nTherefore, the function $f(x)$ has a discontinuity at $x=-2$ because $\\lim_{x\\to (-2)}f(x)\\ne f(-2).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186240809 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1f089e05-0b92-5d54-8257-ef620a1c2e26" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\lim_{x\\to (-\\sqrt{3})}f(x)\\ne f(-\\sqrt{3})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186238317} {:answer/id #uuid "2c117c1e-b599-54be-96b1-26023faa5f11" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$f(x)$ is a piecewise function" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186238447} {:answer/id #uuid "509a042c-0027-580b-8096-62ace31e1b28" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\lim_{x\\to (-\\sqrt{3})^{-}}f(x)\\ne {lim}_{x\\to (-\\sqrt{3})^{+}}f(x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186238752} {:answer/id #uuid "bba9e6d4-ebc8-5a12-9275-5a3db937a009" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$f(-\\sqrt{3})$ does not exist" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186239690} {:answer/id #uuid "f08fffcd-7647-5b3e-99f0-bc5415338449" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$\\lim_{x\\to (-\\sqrt{3})}f(x)$ does not exist" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186240178}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186238317} :answer-field/id #uuid "8734b4ce-4096-5667-a80d-acd673367dd5" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186240569}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "e2dc1316-63b7-56de-ae30-4568e3d25064" :question/math-academy-id "q-104148" :question/problem "For what reason does the function $f(x)$ below have a discontinuity at $x=-\\sqrt{3}?$ \n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{x}^{2}+3}{3}, & x<-\\sqrt{3} \\\\ 3, & x=-\\sqrt{3} \\\\ \\frac{12}{{x}^{2}+3}, & x>-\\sqrt{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Let's check the conditions necessary for $f(x)$ to be continuous at $x=-\\sqrt{3}.$\n\n- $f(-\\sqrt{3})=3,$ so $f(-\\sqrt{3})$ exists. $✓$\n\n- We calculate the one-sided limits and find that they coincide:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to (-\\sqrt{3})^{-}}f(x) & =\\lim_{x\\to (-\\sqrt{3})^{-}}\\left(\\frac{{x}^{2}+3}{3}\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{(-\\sqrt{3})^{2}+3}{3} \\\\  & =2, \\\\ \\lim_{x\\to (-\\sqrt{3})^{+}}f(x) & =\\lim_{x\\to (-\\sqrt{3})^{+}}\\left(\\frac{12}{{x}^{2}+3}\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{12}{(-\\sqrt{3})^{2}+3} \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the overall limit exists and $\\lim_{x\\to (-\\sqrt{3})}f(x)=2.\\,\\,✓$ $\\,\\,\\,$\n\n- The limit of the function is not equal to the function value: $\\lim_{x\\to (-\\sqrt{3})}f(x)=2,$ while $f(-\\sqrt{3})=3.$ $\\times $\n\nTherefore, the function $f(x)$ has a discontinuity at $x=-\\sqrt{3}$ because $\\lim_{x\\to (-\\sqrt{3})}f(x)\\ne f(-\\sqrt{3}).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186244258 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0bfff729-9868-55e5-bed6-9bd735a4c5e0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-90" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186241285} {:answer/id #uuid "28cbbf03-f811-5c25-89c8-95d9751599b1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "30" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186241535} {:answer/id #uuid "423adf75-b13a-524a-95d5-f998d4cfca3e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-60" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186241774} {:answer/id #uuid "6cc8a597-2984-5d30-aeca-06b489945bab" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "45" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242155} {:answer/id #uuid "c18583ef-3a19-572f-aa93-dbec1c50c29f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-50" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242876}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186241285} :answer-field/id #uuid "1e21a9c1-382e-50cc-83b3-0c12c5afd634" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186243497}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "1e0f9240-ada8-57e3-92f8-c3e520c31920" :question/math-academy-id "q-109266" :question/problem "Find $$\n\\displaystyle \\,\\begin{vmatrix}6 & 0 & 2 & 3 \\\\ 0 & x & -1 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 5 & y & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 5\\end{vmatrix}\n$$ given that $$\n\\displaystyle \\,\\begin{vmatrix}x & -1 \\\\ 5 & y\\end{vmatrix}=-3.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Notice that we have a block upper triangular matrix. Therefore, its determinant is equal to the product of the diagonal blocks' determinants:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{vmatrix}6 & 0 & 2 & 3 \\\\ 0 & x & -1 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 5 & y & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 5\\end{vmatrix} & =|\\begin{array}{c|cc|c}6 & 0 & 2 & 3 \\\\ \\hline 0 & x & -1 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 5 & y & 0 \\\\ \\hline 0 & 0 & 0 & 5\\end{array}| \\\\  & =6\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}x & -1 \\\\ 5 & y\\end{vmatrix}\\cdot 5 \\\\  & =6\\cdot (-3)\\cdot 5 \\\\  & =-90.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186244259 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "09b34a75-6e24-525b-b3dd-8940cdc4edb8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186241265} {:answer/id #uuid "486c437e-9a40-5d46-89aa-6c51656cfdf2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "30" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186241835} {:answer/id #uuid "59137118-e001-511f-b69f-3ecb70934b5b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186241982} {:answer/id #uuid "6c307841-6755-575d-94aa-7c93bb6cfdd1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "20" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242151} {:answer/id #uuid "b7adfc2c-ef57-5a51-8511-adbcc4b8b582" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "15" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242788}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186242788} :answer-field/id #uuid "34ce1f74-60fb-554b-a85d-9ebeb0adcded" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186243537}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "548f0040-ec44-5816-9b73-e986b0aab71c" :question/math-academy-id "q-109267" :question/problem "Find $$\n\\displaystyle \\,\\begin{vmatrix}v & w & 0 & 0 \\\\ 5 & 4 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 5 & 5\\end{vmatrix}\n$$ given that $$\n\\displaystyle \\,\\begin{vmatrix}v & w \\\\ 5 & 4\\end{vmatrix}=3.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Notice that we have a block diagonal matrix. Therefore, its determinant is equal to the product of the diagonal blocks' determinants:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{vmatrix}v & w & 0 & 0 \\\\ 5 & 4 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 5 & 5\\end{vmatrix} & =|\\begin{array}{cc|cc}v & w & 0 & 0 \\\\ 5 & 4 & 0 & 0 \\\\ \\hline 0 & 0 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 5 & 5\\end{array}| \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}v & w \\\\ 5 & 4\\end{vmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}2 & 1 \\\\ 5 & 5\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =3\\cdot 5 \\\\  & =15.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186244260 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "217666e8-071d-5a25-aed4-6c71a7bf9bd6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "20" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186241472} {:answer/id #uuid "2b36684f-9b26-5fbd-88f6-07df0bf41f04" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "16" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186241560} {:answer/id #uuid "97ca0a28-ec72-57a9-a810-c783d1f05d5e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "25" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242511} {:answer/id #uuid "a95df6f1-cb88-5478-b26d-eb5cc5b7bce3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "32" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242664} {:answer/id #uuid "ea5b33f4-6f92-5a7f-970e-575f153b4b40" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "24" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186243255}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186243255} :answer-field/id #uuid "39e904b2-301e-59c6-acee-fa2cc4eb472c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186243551}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "6013dd11-16ea-5817-8be8-82c1e6b03532" :question/math-academy-id "q-109270" :question/problem "Find $$\n\\displaystyle \\,\\begin{vmatrix}x & 1 & 10 & 0 \\\\ 2 & y & 0 & 0 \\\\ 5 & 0 & z & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 3\\end{vmatrix}\n$$ given that $$\n\\displaystyle \\,\\begin{vmatrix}x & 1 & 10 \\\\ 2 & y & 0 \\\\ 5 & 0 & z\\end{vmatrix}=8.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Notice that we have a block diagonal matrix. Therefore, its determinant is equal to the product of the diagonal blocks' determinants:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{vmatrix}x & 1 & 10 & 0 \\\\ 2 & y & 0 & 0 \\\\ 5 & 0 & z & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 3\\end{vmatrix} & =|\\begin{array}{ccc|c}x & 1 & 10 & 0 \\\\ 2 & y & 0 & 0 \\\\ 5 & 0 & z & 0 \\\\ \\hline 0 & 0 & 0 & 3\\end{array}| \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}x & 1 & 10 \\\\ 2 & y & 0 \\\\ 5 & 0 & z\\end{vmatrix}\\cdot 3 \\\\  & =8\\cdot 3 \\\\  & =24.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186244261 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "25f5e8bf-d9a2-5262-82f4-44bc899b8243" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-42" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186241508} {:answer/id #uuid "60495048-fc22-52eb-8a50-a12937c5d258" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-72" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242044} {:answer/id #uuid "78133e9e-9b97-506c-a741-ef349995b8a0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-50" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242238} {:answer/id #uuid "8b0472f4-4049-5f20-8651-a9daf5dee95f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "24" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242410} {:answer/id #uuid "be25953e-4e6a-59e9-b509-22380e94c451" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "60" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242850}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186242044} :answer-field/id #uuid "2af4a531-8ea6-5813-b80e-d77b840905e3" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186243515}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "3f16b598-5185-592e-ad1f-83437e5d73ec" :question/math-academy-id "q-109271" :question/problem "Find $$\n\\displaystyle \\,\\begin{vmatrix}4 & 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 3 & m & b & 0 \\\\ 5 & 0 & n & 0 \\\\ 8 & 0 & 0 & 3\\end{vmatrix}\n$$ given that $$\n\\displaystyle \\,\\begin{vmatrix}m & b \\\\ 0 & n\\end{vmatrix}=-6.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Notice that we have a block lower triangular matrix. Therefore, its determinant is equal to the product of the diagonal blocks' determinants:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{vmatrix}4 & 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 3 & m & b & 0 \\\\ 5 & 0 & n & 0 \\\\ 8 & 0 & 0 & 3\\end{vmatrix} & =|\\begin{array}{c|cc|c}4 & 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ \\hline 3 & m & b & 0 \\\\ 5 & 0 & n & 0 \\\\ \\hline 8 & 0 & 0 & 3\\end{array}| \\\\  & =4\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}m & b \\\\ 0 & n\\end{vmatrix}\\cdot 3 \\\\  & =4\\cdot (-6)\\cdot 3 \\\\  & =-72.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186247173 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1900b810-5283-5de2-8515-068de55ed74c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186244536} {:answer/id #uuid "1fd1a474-87d3-51ac-b166-c39933bba5bf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186244596} {:answer/id #uuid "20fcd3e8-ce28-54b8-8247-e60be7ee2cc4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{e}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186244604} {:answer/id #uuid "bd430d41-91d0-5075-8f65-2f92d15b2a80" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186246003} {:answer/id #uuid "e2877088-9bd6-5205-b13a-839003b0bf01" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "e" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186246328}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186246003} :answer-field/id #uuid "ea53fa9d-9912-588b-8d36-17eab032f4b5" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186246965}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "80074266-7584-5c7b-8bab-8678f0fa0b55" :question/math-academy-id "q-110858" :question/problem "The random variables $X$ and $Y$ have the joint probability density function\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}c{e}^{-x-y},\\, & x\\ge 0,\\,y\\ge 0 \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $c$ is a positive real number. Find the value of $c.$" :question/worked-solution "To verify that $f(x,y)$ is a valid joint probability density function, we must check the following conditions:\n\n- $f(x,y)\\ge 0$ for all $(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}$\n\n- $$\n\\displaystyle {∬}_{{R}^{2}}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y=1\n$$\n\nFirst, let's sketch the region $D=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,x\\ge 0,\\,y\\ge 0\\}.$\n\n![](images/99/996bd092be07e2596e30739cc4249e8ed8ef859540067a94e0f9cdd05f9e1f84.png)\n\nNow, let's check our conditions:\n\n- Since $c>0,$ we clearly have $f(x,y)\\ge 0$ for all $(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}.$ So the first condition is satisfied.\n\n- For the second condition to be satisfied, we must have \n\n$$\n{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }c{e}^{-x-y}\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x=1.\n$$\n\nWe evaluate the inner integral by integrating with respect to $y$, treating  $x$ as a constant:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }[{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }c{e}^{-x-y}\\,\\text{d}y]\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }[-c{e}^{-x-y}]_{y=0}^{y=\\infty }\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }c{e}^{-x}\\,\\text{d}x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we integrate with respect to $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }c{e}^{-x}\\,\\text{d}x & =[-c{e}^{-x}]_{0}^{\\infty } \\\\  & =c\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence,\n\n$$\nc=1.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186247174 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3587f4ec-5133-574c-85c2-b625e2022174" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186244789} {:answer/id #uuid "5bfbe54b-2a89-5a19-a15d-418da1e8c98d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186245120} {:answer/id #uuid "840a4e80-3153-50a1-8616-d916253b88ac" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186245477} {:answer/id #uuid "cfc7422d-5fbd-501a-8a53-a1ed60d54f08" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186246170} {:answer/id #uuid "db735a97-9407-5ac8-9622-03d7787e8b2e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186246261}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186245477} :answer-field/id #uuid "cd688132-28be-5fd2-82e8-52d094605862" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186246916}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "90603bab-bd0f-5ff5-b578-328ff040206a" :question/math-academy-id "q-110859" :question/problem "The random variables $X$ and $Y$ have the joint probability density function\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{c}{(1+x+y)^{3}},\\, & x\\ge 0,\\,y\\ge 0 \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $c$ is a positive real number. Find the value of $c.$" :question/worked-solution "To verify that $f(x,y)$ is a valid joint probability density function, we must check the following conditions:\n\n- $f(x,y)\\ge 0$ for all $(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}$\n\n- $$\n\\displaystyle {∬}_{{R}^{2}}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y=1\n$$\n\nFirst, let's sketch the region $D=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,x\\ge 0,\\,y\\ge 0\\}.$\n\n![](images/99/996bd092be07e2596e30739cc4249e8ed8ef859540067a94e0f9cdd05f9e1f84.png)\n\nNow, let's check our conditions:\n\n- Since $c>0,$ we clearly have $f(x,y)\\ge 0$ for all $(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}.$ So the first condition is satisfied.\n\n- For the second condition to be satisfied, we must have \n\n$$\n{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }\\frac{c}{(1+x+y)^{3}}\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x=1.\n$$\n\nWe evaluate the inner integral by integrating with respect to $y$, treating  $x$ as a constant:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }[{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }\\frac{c}{(1+x+y)^{3}}\\,\\text{d}y]\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{[-\\frac{c}{2(1+x+y)^{2}}]}_{y=0}^{y=\\infty }\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }\\frac{c}{2(1+x)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we integrate with respect to $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }\\frac{c}{2(1+x)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x & ={[-\\frac{c}{2(1+x)}]}_{0}^{\\infty } \\\\  & =\\frac{c}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence,\n\n$$\n\\frac{c}{2}=1\\,⇒\\,c=2.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186247175 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "69feed6a-dbe0-5506-a4e6-6b2624ebbe66" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$f$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186245251} {:answer/id #uuid "7db45604-045f-586d-a84a-2cd74a531cf7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$f$ and $g$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186245420} {:answer/id #uuid "90ddcbc6-45ee-5f67-a4a4-2c97b5e22a9e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$f$ and $h$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186245578} {:answer/id #uuid "ae8981f3-eb30-5f81-a243-b408ef0638bf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$g$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186245869} {:answer/id #uuid "b25cc9ae-6119-5fd1-9fcf-65a673d6191e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$g$ and $h$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186245900}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186245420} :answer-field/id #uuid "920732ef-0a8f-5b60-a8af-94d2efc833cf" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186246800}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "29c9baf2-abe1-5681-94f0-fc4fef002362" :question/math-academy-id "q-110862" :question/problem "Let $D=[0,2]\\times [1,2].$ Which of the following functions are valid joint probability density functions?\n\nI. $$\n\\displaystyle f(x,y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{y}{3},\\, & (x,y)\\in D \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nII. $$\n\\displaystyle g(x,y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{x}{2},\\, & (x,y)\\in D \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIII. $$\n\\displaystyle h(x,y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{x-y}{6},\\, & (x,y)\\in D \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "To verify that $f(x,y)$ is a valid joint probability density function, we must check the following conditions:\n\n- $f(x,y)\\ge 0$ for all $(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}$\n\n- $$\n\\displaystyle {∬}_{{R}^{2}}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x\\text{d}y=1\n$$\n\nLet's check the above conditions for each of the given functions.\n\n- The function $f$ is a valid probability density function. Indeed, we have $f(x,y)\\ge 0$ for all $(x,y)\\in {R}^{2},$  and\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{{R}^{2}}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x\\text{d}y & ={∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{2}\\frac{y}{3}\\,\\text{d}x\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}[{\\int }_{0}^{2}\\frac{y}{3}\\,\\text{d}x]\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}{[\\frac{xy}{3}]}_{x=0}^{x=2}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}\\frac{2y}{3}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={[\\frac{{y}^{2}}{3}]}_{1}^{2} \\\\  & =1\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- The function $g$ is a valid probability density function. Indeed, we have $g(x,y)\\ge 0$ for all $(x,y)\\in {R}^{2},$  and\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{{R}^{2}}g(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x\\text{d}y & ={∬}_{D}g(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{2}\\frac{x}{2}\\,\\text{d}x\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}[{\\int }_{0}^{2}\\frac{x}{2}\\,\\text{d}x]\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}{[\\frac{{x}^{2}}{4}]}_{x=0}^{x=2}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}1\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =[y]_{1}^{2} \\\\  & =1\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- The function $h$ is *not* a valid probability density function because it does not satisfy the first condition. For example,\n\n$$\nh(0,2)=\\frac{0-2}{6}=-\\frac{1}{3}≱0.\\,\\times \n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"$f$ and $g$ only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186247177 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0d30e286-efc5-5ff3-9660-b088d67bd07e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186244433} {:answer/id #uuid "527a2a91-877d-5115-a2d6-fdc13762c9de" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186245042} {:answer/id #uuid "9e095984-e455-50cc-a19c-a52e73b4ee8e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186245723} {:answer/id #uuid "a85ea72b-0587-5ab3-8ded-c7e8907e17af" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186245818} {:answer/id #uuid "ecd6ca77-0039-5568-99cc-b60df6fbb225" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186246408}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186244433} :answer-field/id #uuid "b42dc502-18a9-510a-a154-ac90ca8fbcc6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186246875}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "b563caf4-163e-5568-8f80-cdcffafdc12c" :question/math-academy-id "q-110868" :question/problem "The random variables $X$ and $Y$ have the joint probability density function\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}c\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x+y),\\, & 0\\le y\\le x\\le \\frac{\\pi }{2}, \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $c$ is a positive real number. Find the value of $c.$" :question/worked-solution "To verify that $f(x,y)$ is a valid joint probability density function, we must check the following conditions:\n\n- $f(x,y)\\ge 0$ for all $(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}$\n\n- $$\n\\displaystyle {∬}_{{R}^{2}}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x\\text{d}y=1\n$$\n\nFirst, let's sketch the region $D=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le y\\le x\\le \\frac{\\pi }{2}\\}.$\n\n![](images/a4/a49e679b3919594edc7fe963190a754324b9b5ea980d1936121c040d0ee52284.png)\n\nNow, let's check our conditions:\n\n- Since $c>0,$ we clearly have $f(x,y)\\ge 0$ for all $(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}.$ So the first condition is satisfied.\n\n- For the second condition to be satisfied, we must have \n\n$$\n{∬}_{{R}^{2}}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x\\text{d}y={∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x\\text{d}y={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{\\int }_{0}^{x}c\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x+y)\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x=1.\n$$\n\nW\nWe evaluate the inner integral by integrating with respect to $y$, treating  $x$ as a constant:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}[{\\int }_{0}^{x}c\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x+y)\\,\\text{d}y]\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{[-c\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x+y)]}_{y=0}^{y=x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}(-c\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x+c\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)\\,\\text{d}x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we integrate with respect to $x$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}(c\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-c\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x)\\,\\text{d}x & ={[c\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-\\frac{c\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x}{2}]}_{0}^{\\pi /2} \\\\  & =c\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)-\\frac{c\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi )}{2} \\\\  & =c\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence,\n\n$$\nc=1.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186247189 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1002abf0-16a9-545d-9373-728ba3f94fcc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{e+{e}^{-1}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186244461} {:answer/id #uuid "5d4e204d-0b4c-54ba-87b4-56d60185143a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{e-{e}^{-1}+2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186245138} {:answer/id #uuid "6801d7b6-a3db-5055-923c-7f3de5384e16" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{e-{e}^{-1}-2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186245235} {:answer/id #uuid "b2bebd39-305e-5fff-9a73-443b09049842" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{e+{e}^{-1}-2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186245903} {:answer/id #uuid "cb61f56d-fad9-5c38-98e3-ebb91e331c3e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{e+{e}^{-1}+2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186246125}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186245903} :answer-field/id #uuid "8671c1fd-5e35-5984-8241-cdbb795f5459" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186246781}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "e5fb35e0-0098-50b2-92bd-fb03dcef42bb" :question/math-academy-id "q-110977" :question/problem "The random variables $X$ and $Y$ have the joint probability density function\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}c{e}^{x-y},\\, & 0\\le x<2y\\le 2, \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $c$ is a positive real number. Find the value of $c.$" :question/worked-solution "To verify that $f(x,y)$ is a valid joint probability density function, we must check the following conditions:\n\n- $f(x,y)\\ge 0$ for all $(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}$\n\n- $$\n\\displaystyle {∬}_{{R}^{2}}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x\\text{d}y=1\n$$\n\nFirst, let's sketch the region $D=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le x<2y\\le 2\\}.$\n\n![](images/60/60acb4126a456e51e00db8bbeb2f8dbc6a773a4792dda7838d2aa6a3553fa739.png)\n\nNow, let's check our conditions:\n\n- Since $c>0,$ we clearly have $f(x,y)\\ge 0$ for all $(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}.$ So the first condition is satisfied.\n\n- For the second condition to be satisfied, we must have \n\n$$\n{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{2y}c{e}^{x-y}\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y=1.\n$$\n\n$$\n{∬}_{{R}^{2}}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x\\text{d}y={∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x\\text{d}y={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{2y}c{e}^{x-y}\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y=1.\n$$\n\nWe evaluate the inner integral by integrating with respect to $x$, treating  $y$ as a constant:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{2y}c{e}^{x-y}\\,dx\\,dy & =c{\\int }_{0}^{1}{e}^{-y}[{\\int }_{0}^{2y}{e}^{x}\\,dx]dy \\\\  & =c{\\int }_{0}^{1}{e}^{-y}[{e}^{x}]_{x=0}^{x=2y}\\,dy \\\\  & =c{\\int }_{0}^{1}{e}^{-y}({e}^{2y}-{e}^{0})dy \\\\  & =c{\\int }_{0}^{1}({e}^{y}-{e}^{-y})\\,dy\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we integrate with respect to $y$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}c{\\int }_{0}^{1}({e}^{y}-{e}^{-y})\\,dy & =c[{e}^{y}+{e}^{-y}]_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =c({e}^{1}+{e}^{-1})-({e}^{0}+{e}^{0}) \\\\  & =c(e+{e}^{-1}-2)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence,\n\n$$\nc(e+{e}^{-1}-2)=1\\,⇒\\,c=\\frac{1}{e+{e}^{-1}-2}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186247193 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7262c3f0-8d17-5510-940c-dfd096f6e29b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186245334} {:answer/id #uuid "a052dbb7-5c7d-5743-ab74-b24d3428a419" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{12}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186245739} {:answer/id #uuid "b86aa479-c5c2-5a5d-a05d-a655be0a0795" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186245963} {:answer/id #uuid "c4391b2b-a3c5-5ab3-93fa-4120a6ab86ae" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186246058} {:answer/id #uuid "f0571465-3cc4-52ab-8fdc-d935dbfeecab" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186246440}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186245334} :answer-field/id #uuid "70f04b94-d5fc-5445-a232-b2fc977bdc31" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186246745}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "bf819c75-7419-5272-b743-a86ae3746c07" :question/math-academy-id "q-110985" :question/problem "The random variables $X$ and $Y$ have the joint probability density function\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}c(1-xy),\\, & {y}^{2}\\le x\\le y, \\\\ 0,\\, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \nwhere $c$ is a positive real number. Find the value of $c.$" :question/worked-solution "To verify that $f(x,y)$ is a valid joint probability density function, we must check the following conditions:\n\n- $f(x,y)\\ge 0$ for all $(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}$\n\n- $$\n\\displaystyle {∬}_{{R}^{2}}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x\\text{d}y=1\n$$\n\nFirst, let's sketch the region $D=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,{y}^{2}\\le x\\le y\\}.$\n\n![](images/1e/1ef0c7b289e24a98366c64b9a213c4f67d2fc63157b3319f0118eb16c514097e.png)\n\nNow, let's check our conditions:\n\n- Since $c>0,$ we clearly have $f(x,y)\\ge 0$ for all $(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}.$ So the first condition is satisfied.\n\n- For the second condition to be satisfied, we must have \n\n$$\n{∬}_{{R}^{2}}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x\\text{d}y={∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x\\text{d}y={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{{y}^{2}}^{y}c(1-xy)\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y=1.\n$$\n\nWe evaluate the inner integral by integrating with respect to $x$, treating  $y$ as a constant:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{{y}^{2}}^{y}c(1-xy)\\,\\text{d}x\\text{d}y & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{[cx-\\frac{cy{x}^{2}}{2}]}_{x={y}^{2}}^{x=y}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}c(y-\\frac{{y}^{3}}{2}-({y}^{2}-\\frac{{y}^{5}}{2}))\\,dy \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}c(y-\\frac{{y}^{3}}{2}-{y}^{2}+\\frac{{y}^{5}}{2})\\,dy\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we integrate with respect to $y$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{1}c(y-\\frac{{y}^{3}}{2}-{y}^{2}+\\frac{{y}^{5}}{2})\\,dy & =c{[\\frac{{y}^{2}}{2}-\\frac{{y}^{4}}{8}-\\frac{{y}^{3}}{3}+\\frac{{y}^{6}}{12}]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{c}{2}-\\frac{c}{8}-\\frac{c}{3}+\\frac{c}{12}-0 \\\\  & =\\frac{c}{8}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence,\n\n$$\n\\frac{c}{8}=1\\,⇒\\,c=8.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186247194 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "083c51fc-aa18-56dc-b3c0-124a775f8946" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186244378} {:answer/id #uuid "675427f6-ac1f-521a-b7bc-59f1e3b0b6a8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186245221} {:answer/id #uuid "714d2134-55a2-57cb-8d39-8bf99301077a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186245324} {:answer/id #uuid "98716022-d6bf-54d3-874d-c54fc12b52e2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186245665} {:answer/id #uuid "ba1eb9d4-e845-50fa-86d1-d1a588f1aaf2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186245976}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186244378} :answer-field/id #uuid "ae9c6475-f39e-5cd6-88f8-b618ffddc3b9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186246865}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "800aa4d6-ad4a-5a10-9d04-aca005ce255a" :question/math-academy-id "q-110986" :question/problem "The random variables $X$ and $Y$ have the joint probability density function\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{c}{yx\\operatorname{ln}⁡y},\\, & 1\\le x\\le e,\\,e\\le y\\le {e}^{x}, \\\\ 0,\\, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \nwhere $c$ is a positive real number. Find the value of $c.$" :question/worked-solution "To verify that $f(x,y)$ is a valid joint probability density function, we must check the following conditions:\n\n- $f(x,y)\\ge 0$ for all $(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}$\n\n- $$\n\\displaystyle {∬}_{{R}^{2}}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x\\text{d}y=1\n$$\n\nFirst, let's sketch the region $D=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,1\\le x\\le e,\\,e\\le y\\le {e}^{x}\\}.$\n\n![](images/26/268e469b0306d22e6b6dc05905a9d56a1cabd4b0e405f741ff458ca241440d4a.png)\n\nNow, let's check our conditions:\n\n- Since $c>0,$ we clearly have $f(x,y)\\ge 0$ for all $(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}.$ So the first condition is satisfied.\n\n- For the second condition to be satisfied, we must have \n\n$$\n{\\int }_{1}^{e}{\\int }_{e}^{{e}^{x}}\\frac{c}{yx\\operatorname{ln}⁡y}\\,dy\\,dx=1.\n$$\n\n$$\n{∬}_{{R}^{2}}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x\\text{d}y={∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x\\text{d}y={\\int }_{1}^{e}{\\int }_{e}^{{e}^{x}}\\frac{c}{yx\\operatorname{ln}⁡y}\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x=1.\n$$\n\nWe evaluate the inner integral by integrating with respect to $y$, treating  $x$ as a constant:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{e}{\\int }_{e}^{{e}^{x}}\\frac{c}{yx\\operatorname{ln}⁡y}\\,dy\\,dx & ={\\int }_{1}^{e}\\frac{c}{x}[{\\int }_{e}^{{e}^{x}}\\frac{1}{y\\operatorname{ln}⁡y}\\,dy]\\,dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{e}\\frac{c}{x}[\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\operatorname{ln}⁡y)]_{y=e}^{y={e}^{x}}\\,dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{e}\\frac{c}{x}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\operatorname{ln}⁡{e}^{x})-\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\operatorname{ln}⁡e))\\,dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{e}\\frac{c\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}{x}dx\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we integrate with respect to $x$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{e}\\frac{c\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}{x}dx & ={[\\frac{c{ln}^{2}⁡x}{2}]}_{1}^{e} \\\\  & =\\frac{c{ln}^{2}⁡e}{2}-\\frac{c{ln}^{2}⁡1}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{c}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence,\n\n$$\n\\frac{c}{2}=1\\,⇒\\,c=2.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186247195 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3d95a2b2-2cae-509d-8832-080d1868eab1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186244856} {:answer/id #uuid "909b217b-25e2-5467-b502-a39cc113f4d3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186245574} {:answer/id #uuid "9b24761b-d3c2-5a10-945a-f25827f8e11b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186245685} {:answer/id #uuid "e11294d5-0034-5c0b-b005-ade253f9db8a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{6}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186246320} {:answer/id #uuid "f92cd467-275f-5a82-8fce-90184930514b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186246506}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186246320} :answer-field/id #uuid "4116de07-36f6-57aa-833f-030f2b4212ae" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186246654}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "f09c6e4e-1ed7-59e2-b591-ad3f2f59a93f" :question/math-academy-id "q-110987" :question/problem "The random variables $X$ and $Y$ have the joint probability density function\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}cx{e}^{y},\\, & 1\\le x\\le 2,\\,0\\le y\\le \\operatorname{ln}⁡x, \\\\ 0,\\, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \nwhere $c$ is a positive real number. Find the value of $c.$" :question/worked-solution "To verify that $f(x,y)$ is a valid joint probability density function, we must check the following conditions:\n\n- $f(x,y)\\ge 0$ for all $(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}$\n\n- $$\n\\displaystyle {∬}_{{R}^{2}}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x\\text{d}y=1\n$$\n\nFirst, let's sketch the region $D=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,1\\le x\\le 2,\\,0\\le y\\le \\operatorname{ln}⁡x\\}.$\n\n![](images/8a/8a94024cbce0389b1d9c60bf64a38cb7c8e8e52836b2b39e9e638f856a1c5269.png)\n\nNow, let's check our conditions:\n\n- Since $c>0,$ we clearly have $f(x,y)\\ge 0$ for all $(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}.$ So the first condition is satisfied.\n\n- For the second condition to be satisfied, we must have \n\n$$\n{∬}_{{R}^{2}}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x\\text{d}y={∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x\\text{d}y={\\int }_{1}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}cx{e}^{y}\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x=1.\n$$\n\nWe evaluate the inner integral by integrating with respect to $y$, treating  $x$ as a constant:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}cx{e}^{y}\\,dy\\,dx & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}cx[{\\int }_{0}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}{e}^{y}\\,dy]dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}cx[{e}^{y}]_{y=0}^{y=\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}cx({e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}-{e}^{0})dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}cx(x-1)dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}(c{x}^{2}-cx)dx\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we integrate with respect to $x$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{2}(c{x}^{2}-cx)dx & ={[\\frac{c{x}^{3}}{3}-\\frac{c{x}^{2}}{2}]}_{1}^{2} \\\\  & =(\\frac{8c}{3}-\\frac{4c}{2})-(\\frac{c}{3}-\\frac{c}{2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{5c}{6}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence,\n\n$$\n\\frac{5c}{6}=1\\,⇒\\,c=\\frac{6}{5}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186247209 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5b271801-fe40-5d86-a7a8-da0a2a41d256" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186245111} {:answer/id #uuid "702f1852-5d9a-54f2-b897-ef6ff52fc2f8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186245317} {:answer/id #uuid "a1a1db5a-42aa-5bf5-bb2a-55d204ec594c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{16}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186245751} {:answer/id #uuid "d976eb30-d7a3-522e-8754-a28f49f82292" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{32}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186246247} {:answer/id #uuid "faa67357-2cdf-517d-b66c-41be3a0be6c7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186246512}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186246512} :answer-field/id #uuid "6516c3a0-72a2-5a42-b1f2-9eb300e6d5cd" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186246723}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "9ffe233c-16f5-535b-b931-9450db9f86d9" :question/math-academy-id "q-111150" :question/problem "The random variables $X$ and $Y$ have the joint probability density function\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{xy}{4},\\, & 0\\le x\\le 2,\\,0\\le y\\le 2, \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFind $P(X<Y).$" :question/worked-solution "If $f(x,y)$ is a joint probability density function for the random variables $X$ and $Y$ and $A⊂{R}^{2},$ then\n\n$$\nP((X,Y)\\in A)={∬}_{A}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x\\text{d}y.\n$$\n\nNotice that $f(x,y)$ is nonnegative in the region $D=[0,2]\\times [0,2],$ and the event $A=\\{X<Y\\}$ corresponds to a subset of $D,$ shown as the shaded region below:\n\n![](images/92/9213a4b132f625677fcc5719e71900d037ac08088dc477f8d6a93f9ca67bc3d4.png)\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X<Y) & ={∬}_{A}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{y}\\frac{xy}{4}\\,\\text{d}x\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}[{\\int }_{0}^{y}\\frac{xy}{4}\\,\\text{d}x]\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}{[\\frac{{x}^{2}y}{8}]}_{x=0}^{x=y}\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}\\frac{{y}^{3}}{8}\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={[\\frac{{y}^{4}}{32}]}_{0}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186247211 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "46e00eda-4098-5ea8-9286-1c659780d381" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.417" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186244945} {:answer/id #uuid "794adeac-c0b1-576d-a547-3cbb8fd1ea50" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.548" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186245382} {:answer/id #uuid "79ca5e50-3240-55d0-b787-d6e3ab9623b0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.613" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186245387} {:answer/id #uuid "8acdfd26-6153-50a4-b247-82e1a51c91b2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.827" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186245530} {:answer/id #uuid "fc2f6765-7324-55dd-8d67-cc200672047c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.793" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186246522}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186246522} :answer-field/id #uuid "e46a81bb-da04-58df-8b73-5a6b912925ee" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186246952}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "70065778-db0a-5680-ada4-1a1c95f48e99" :question/math-academy-id "q-111162" :question/problem "The random variables $X$ and $Y$ have the joint probability density function\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x+y),\\, & 0\\le x\\le y\\le \\frac{\\pi }{2}, \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFind $P(X\\le \\frac{\\pi }{4}).$ Round your answer to $3$ decimal places." :question/worked-solution "If $f(x,y)$ is a joint probability density function for the random variables $X$ and $Y$ and $A⊂{R}^{2},$ then\n\n$$\nP((X,Y)\\in A)={∬}_{A}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x\\text{d}y.\n$$\n\nNotice that $f(x,y)$ is nonnegative in the region $D=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le y\\le x\\le \\frac{\\pi }{2}\\},$ and the event $A=\\{X\\le \\frac{\\pi }{4}\\}$ corresponds to a subset of $D,$ shown as the shaded region below:\n\n![](images/ea/ea585fb6e12672f5cc854578cb12ff0357cfcd8d2f3d3b75a82723241cdaf3d4.png)\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X\\le \\frac{\\pi }{4}) & ={∬}_{A}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}{\\int }_{x}^{\\pi /2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x+y)\\,\\text{d}y\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}[{\\int }_{x}^{\\pi /2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x+y)\\,\\text{d}y]\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}[-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x+y)]_{y=x}^{y=\\pi /2}\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x+\\frac{\\pi }{2}))\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={[\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x+\\frac{\\pi }{2})]}_{0}^{\\pi /4} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}-0+1 \\\\  & =\\frac{3-\\sqrt{2}}{2} \\\\  & ≈0.793.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186247212 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "29acc45f-d9a2-5198-b52c-9661da5934be" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186244682} {:answer/id #uuid "95f053d5-8795-52c1-b722-00db9f29acbc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4}{27}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186245630} {:answer/id #uuid "b23c800d-72d5-5790-993e-23742300ba63" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4}{9}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186245898} {:answer/id #uuid "c7e30e05-845a-5458-9e2a-b3871b7cd5e9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186246081} {:answer/id #uuid "f80a92e0-f18c-5c27-833b-a6c8d19d4085" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{9}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186246493}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186245898} :answer-field/id #uuid "27ad5964-4ae5-57fc-9939-768924881d76" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186246610}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "8656cfbb-6643-57f2-9ab0-17cded63f444" :question/math-academy-id "q-111163" :question/problem "The random variables $X$ and $Y$ have the joint probability density function\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{4}{(1+x+y)^{3}},\\, & 0\\le y\\le x, \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFind $P(X+Y<2).$" :question/worked-solution "If $f(x,y)$ is a joint probability density function for the random variables $X$ and $Y$ and $A⊂{R}^{2},$ then\n\n$$\nP((X,Y)\\in A)={∬}_{A}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x\\text{d}y.\n$$\n\nNotice that $f(x,y)$ is nonnegative in the region $D=\\{0\\le y\\le x\\},$ and the event $A=\\{X+Y<2\\}$ corresponds to a subset of $D,$ shown as the shaded region below:\n\n![](images/f7/f7a0f0fb9d20a4b88d0aa4c395e2adb1d62195601ce756f57a48becc6ab38681.png)\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X+Y<2) & ={∬}_{A}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{y}^{2-y}\\frac{4}{(1+x+y)^{3}}\\,\\text{d}x\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}[{\\int }_{y}^{2-y}\\frac{4}{(1+x+y)^{3}}\\,\\text{d}x]\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{[-\\frac{2}{(1+x+y)^{2}}]}_{x=y}^{x=2-y}\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(\\frac{2}{(1+2y)^{2}}-\\frac{2}{9})\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={[-\\frac{1}{1+2y}-\\frac{2y}{9}]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{3}-\\frac{2}{9}+1 \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{9}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186247213 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2f927547-7aa7-5076-8d36-ac157d2b457c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.75" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186244741} {:answer/id #uuid "5780513f-168d-5408-a894-514183892c66" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186245085} {:answer/id #uuid "96f1be30-a397-5e4f-a5fc-6ac756ff7738" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186245645} {:answer/id #uuid "b2c65bdf-5bd7-5455-8b12-4605a010ab42" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186245904} {:answer/id #uuid "bc008801-a322-5a70-a5f4-032dd023a42b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186245989}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186245989} :answer-field/id #uuid "f50bb897-e2b5-507e-974e-a5e6f1881a90" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186246986}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "5199c42f-aff1-55e5-b62b-a83ffcaa9370" :question/math-academy-id "q-111167" :question/problem "The random variables $X$ and $Y$ have the joint probability density function\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}1,\\, & 0\\le x\\le 1,\\,0\\le y\\le 1, \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFind $P(X+Y>1).$" :question/worked-solution "If $f(x,y)$ is a joint probability density function for the random variables $X$ and $Y$ and $A⊂{R}^{2},$ then\n\n$$\nP((X,Y)\\in A)={∬}_{A}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x\\text{d}y.\n$$\n\nNotice that $f(x,y)$ is nonnegative in the region $D=[0,1]\\times [0,1],$ and the event $A=\\{X+Y>1\\}$ corresponds to a subset of $D,$ shown as the shaded region below:\n\n![](images/58/58c1beb1b13680e330260d93fdf8e60460e2bfeae4c1cbbc2fce8b261afe7ea1.png)\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X+Y>1) & ={∬}_{A}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={∬}_{A}\\,\\text{d}x\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\text{Area}(A) \\\\  & =0.5.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186247214 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "30e1a5e1-53dd-5405-b773-5096a99bef72" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186244752} {:answer/id #uuid "45642459-3fe9-5e9f-a372-085a1f846f33" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186244929} {:answer/id #uuid "5648d231-b525-59ab-a5e8-de881d67e6c3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186245074} {:answer/id #uuid "8228192e-4fc9-5017-8e03-1a47205cf78d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186245463} {:answer/id #uuid "daf40184-bf74-5552-b396-a2c8bef81594" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186246260}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186244752} :answer-field/id #uuid "ea68a650-57c2-5481-9fd2-8ae6a75ff93a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186246966}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "bdc40edd-ea5e-57db-9e7a-f82ff5703a98" :question/math-academy-id "q-111168" :question/problem "The random variables $X$ and $Y$ have the joint probability density function\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2},\\, & 0\\le x\\le 2,\\,0\\le y\\le 1, \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFind $P(X>1).$" :question/worked-solution "If $f(x,y)$ is a joint probability density function for the random variables $X$ and $Y$ and $A⊂{R}^{2},$ then\n\n$$\nP((X,Y)\\in A)={∬}_{A}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x\\text{d}y.\n$$\n\nNotice that $f(x,y)$ is nonnegative in the region $D=[0,2]\\times [0,1],$ and the event $A=\\{X>1\\}$ corresponds to a subset of $D,$ shown as the shaded region below:\n\n![](images/26/26cbd44827c3a5a3fde5d61b631ce4f214bcf9a36ce6bf76129b1fe9f9478eff.png)\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X>1) & ={∬}_{A}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={∬}_{A}\\frac{1}{2}\\,\\text{d}x\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\text{Area}(A) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262576 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0e12cbba-3631-54d1-84d7-964542214c91" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{-3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260005} {:answer/id #uuid "807da1aa-15ec-597d-9ce5-8ad32b6f43db" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3{e}^{-3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260993} {:answer/id #uuid "96728374-4c7e-5993-8514-03f9bcc34be0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{-\\frac{1}{3}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261179} {:answer/id #uuid "b9d9e748-5a09-5259-ae66-914ca28c408d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{-2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261503} {:answer/id #uuid "c42d6103-4d6c-587b-81f1-697d7b6da448" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{-1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261604}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186261604} :answer-field/id #uuid "c33853e1-0a4c-5b0b-b820-5e3dbbe949b2" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186262474}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "e72b6d0d-48ab-5023-98ff-ea2cdd5bb4d7" :question/math-academy-id "q-126016" :question/problem "Compute $P(X\\ge 3)$ given that the random variable $X$ has the probability density function\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{3}{e}^{-\\frac{1}{3}x},\\, & x\\ge 0, \\\\ 0,\\, & x<0.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "If a continuous random variable $X$ has the probability density function $f(x),$ then \n\n$$\nP(a<X<b)={\\int }_{a}^{b}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nSo, in our case, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X\\ge 3) & =P(3\\le X<\\infty ) \\\\  & ={\\int }_{3}^{\\infty }f(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{3}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{3}{e}^{-\\frac{1}{3}x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={[-{e}^{-\\frac{1}{3}x}]}_{3}^{\\infty } \\\\  & ={e}^{-1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262579 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "183da1c9-917b-5fdc-b6b6-17ca2b4b1566" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260086} {:answer/id #uuid "9279ff2b-bdd6-558b-b9f7-b1a54f7eb5c8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{9}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261147} {:answer/id #uuid "bc7cc726-f0c3-5809-bccc-bc08d0e00da0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261535} {:answer/id #uuid "f494a86e-8c02-500a-abe4-99ca5857e464" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186262012} {:answer/id #uuid "f4c1dfbd-6c27-5075-b9c4-913e834f4273" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186262014}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186261535} :answer-field/id #uuid "8b77115e-fce9-56b5-b8af-ef860125ad2a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186262370}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "74467f4e-ddb0-5476-baee-aa2c6186829d" :question/math-academy-id "q-126031" :question/problem "Compute $P(X>2)$ given that the random variable $X$ has the probability density function\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{(1+x)^{2}},\\, & x\\ge 0, \\\\ 0,\\, & \\text{otherwise.}\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "If a continuous random variable $X$ has the probability density function $f(x),$ then \n\n$$\nP(a<X<b)={\\int }_{a}^{b}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nSo, in our case, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X>2) & =P(2<X<\\infty ) \\\\  & ={\\int }_{2}^{\\infty }f(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{2}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{(1+x)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={[-\\frac{1}{x+1}]}_{2}^{\\infty } \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186265765 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "000fd319-21cd-5932-83a1-59aa9a87a731" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186463766} {:answer/id #uuid "ef9a3c6c-59a7-5af0-8a47-b00453e5ad3c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186463767}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186463766} :answer-field/id #uuid "bcfd5bd0-78ee-5e58-a029-75f3bef6e4e0" :answer-field/key "$R=[-1,3]\\times [-2,1]$" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186464817} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c7581893-4afd-58a5-bc3f-c09a16f23deb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186463768} {:answer/id #uuid "be899893-b4ad-50b0-8670-5d5bc7b0e630" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186463769}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186463768} :answer-field/id #uuid "60c6461b-b5d1-5113-8d72-de726845724a" :answer-field/key "$R=[-1,2]\\times [-2,2]$" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186464818} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "76b528d7-b981-5937-928c-b87c5b84aa24" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186463770} {:answer/id #uuid "bedec7e3-9e92-5924-ab1e-9861d797ebf1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186463771}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186463771} :answer-field/id #uuid "fb7b5924-cef6-5250-b973-935f3b5e537c" :answer-field/key "$R=[-2,3]\\times [-2,2]$" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186464819}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "16d77304-e003-57b3-942e-c991453c4142" :question/math-academy-id "q-129658" :question/problem "![](images/b9/b946843e712c6294e261cb3015b02bc065cd265763805cf046009a2db0d11b83.png)\n\nConsider the region $D$ shown above. If \n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}{e}^{x+y}, & (x,y)\\in R\\cap D \\\\ 0, & (x,y)\\in R∖D\\end{aligned}\\,\\text{and}\\,{∬}_{D}{e}^{x+y}\\,\\text{d}A={∬}_{R}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A,\n$$\n\nwhich of the following could be the definition of the rectangular domain $R?$\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "Notice that if $R$ covers $D$ completely and we define \n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}{e}^{x+y}, & (x,y)\\in R\\cap D \\\\ 0, & (x,y)\\in R∖D,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwe obtain\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}{e}^{x+y}\\,\\text{d}A={∬}_{R}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each of the given rectangles.\n\n- Rectangles $R=[-1,3]\\times [-2,1]$ and $R=[-1,2]\\times [-2,2]$ do not cover the region $D.$\n\n- Rectangle $R=[-2,3]\\times [-2,2]$ covers the region $D$ completely.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is $R=[-2,3]\\times [-2,2]$ only."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186265783 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "74a8a907-3b80-5bad-9a29-30a21856511a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186463802} {:answer/id #uuid "e0530774-b067-5d2a-af75-a41f92c3bbd4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186463803}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186463802} :answer-field/id #uuid "5c83706e-2a89-572b-be4f-53b331e0da94" :answer-field/key "$\\{\\begin{aligned}3{x}^{2}-5{y}^{4}, & (x,y)\\in R∖D \\\\ 0, & (x,y)\\in D∖R\\end{aligned}$" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186464835} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b1a8dad4-1bdd-5443-9ac4-4fc8b8d8e996" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186463804} {:answer/id #uuid "de0f066f-9fe8-50fc-8134-3d378e99638a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186463805}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186463805} :answer-field/id #uuid "e035c698-b962-54b0-92c5-c4c2b071a44b" :answer-field/key "$\\{\\begin{aligned}0, & (x,y)\\in R∖D \\\\ 3{x}^{2}-5{y}^{4}, & (x,y)\\in D\\end{aligned}$" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186464836} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f75f4318-9008-5519-a37d-82bc83242534" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186463806} {:answer/id #uuid "327deeeb-feab-50fa-ae12-defb39a7e5f3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186463807}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186463806} :answer-field/id #uuid "1698b42c-e9f9-5ecb-ac8b-6e702704c7f8" :answer-field/key "$\\{\\begin{aligned}0, & (x,y)\\in R\\cap D \\\\ 3{x}^{2}-5{y}^{4}, & (x,y)\\in R∖D\\end{aligned}$" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186464837} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "21d6fea4-9eb4-5abf-b350-d89085e3deba" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186667944} {:answer/id #uuid "423de72a-dce3-5896-b928-c63a465696bd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186667945}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186667944} :answer-field/id #uuid "3ed4010e-72e1-5ada-8a09-3bbfb5feecfd" :answer-field/key "None of the above" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186668249}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "d2768639-7882-5bba-9579-987ae66a0169" :question/math-academy-id "q-129904" :question/problem "![](images/f8/f8cb975b622bfe420d9418587293e83de60c297019f15e1b379bc118e079d35d.png)\n\nConsider the region $D$ shown above. If $R=[-1,2]\\times [-2,2],$ and\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}(3{x}^{2}-5{y}^{4})\\,\\text{d}A={∬}_{R}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A,\n$$\n\nwhich of the following could be the definition of $f(x,y)?$\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "First, let's draw our region $D$ and rectangle $R:$\n\n![](images/89/8944afb608465e49c7ae253f8e2518aa3d43b1c6e121dd91178ca1a67116a508.png)\n\nNotice that the rectangle $R$ contains the region $D$ completely. \n\nNow, if we define $f(x,y)$ to be equal $3{x}^{2}-5{y}^{4}$ on $D$ and to be equal $0$ outside $D,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}(3{x}^{2}-5{y}^{4})\\,\\text{d}A={∬}_{R}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each of the given functions.\n\n- We cannot use functions $$\n\\displaystyle \\{\\begin{aligned}3{x}^{2}-5{y}^{4}, & (x,y)\\in R∖D \\\\ 0, & (x,y)\\in D∖R\\end{aligned}\n$$ and $$\n\\displaystyle \\{\\begin{aligned}0, & (x,y)\\in R\\cap D \\\\ 3{x}^{2}-5{y}^{4}, & (x,y)\\in R∖D\\end{aligned}.\n$$ Both of these functions are not identically zero for all points in $R\\,∖D.$ For example, $(x,y)=(2,2)\\in R\\,∖\\,D,$ but the functions are nonzero at this point.\n\n- Function $$\n\\displaystyle \\{\\begin{aligned}0, & (x,y)\\in R∖D \\\\ 3{x}^{2}-5{y}^{4}, & (x,y)\\in D\\end{aligned}\n$$ equals $3{x}^{2}-5{y}^{4}$ on $D$ and equals $0$ outside $D.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is $$\n\\displaystyle \\{\\begin{aligned}0, & (x,y)\\in R∖D \\\\ 3{x}^{2}-5{y}^{4}, & (x,y)\\in D\\end{aligned}\n$$ only."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186265803 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "073c5c5e-c861-57e9-945f-a83adfdf8a69" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.0518" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263064} {:answer/id #uuid "3aa6281c-b856-56ea-8483-d472bdd43f71" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.1296" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263523} {:answer/id #uuid "69ea2f8a-af95-5a4d-8645-604fba8ea7fc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.0311" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263909} {:answer/id #uuid "e8ecdb94-be51-5a12-b9e7-329cf17fb4db" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.0154" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186265026} {:answer/id #uuid "fa278776-416e-538f-902a-3da3aa401ad1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.0778" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186265191}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186263064} :answer-field/id #uuid "ae15d814-eae8-5922-bc14-909a3665db99" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186265555}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "e412f016-1827-5a09-955d-1fa10d7d3776" :question/math-academy-id "q-130031" :question/problem "Given $X∼\\text{Geom}(0.4),$ compute $P(X=5).$  Round your final answer to $4$ decimal places." :question/worked-solution "If $X∼\\text{Geom}(p),$ then $X$ has the following probability mass function:\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}(1-p)^{x-1}p, & x=1,2,3,… \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHere, $X∼\\text{Geom}(0.4),$ so the distribution of $X$ in this case is\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}{(0.6)}^{x-1}(0.4), & x=1,2,3,… \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X=5) & =f(5) \\\\  & ={(0.6)}^{4}(0.4) \\\\  & ≈0.0518.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186265804 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6e35ef35-ff48-5e0e-b45c-16d9c9d6eb80" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{∬}_{(1,2)\\times (1,2)}\\frac{y+1}{1+{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A+{∬}_{(1,2)\\times (2,4)}\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263953} {:answer/id #uuid "c3308c3c-38ac-5e02-8bb3-09f266686a29" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{∬}_{(1,2)\\times (1,4)}(\\frac{y+1}{1+{x}^{2}}+\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{y}^{2}})\\,\\text{d}A" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186264701} {:answer/id #uuid "eb6905fa-dfd2-5209-96b6-0a3467859dae" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{∬}_{(1,2)\\times (1,2)}\\frac{y+1}{1+{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A+{∬}_{(2,4)\\times (2,4)}\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186265055} {:answer/id #uuid "ec02c144-31a5-5879-a3d0-81ee338bad48" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{∬}_{(1,2)\\times (1,4)}\\frac{{x}^{2}(y+1)}{{y}^{2}(1+{x}^{2})}\\,\\text{d}A" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186265065} {:answer/id #uuid "f38d6811-6741-50d2-bbf2-973c0069b778" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{∬}_{(1,2)\\times (1,2)}\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A+{∬}_{(1,2)\\times (2,4)}\\frac{y+1}{1+{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186265139}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186263953} :answer-field/id #uuid "88b62c52-192d-530f-aa19-1533d59dee25" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186265486}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "4097ab0e-1da6-536a-8ecd-ac95516f4e76" :question/math-academy-id "q-130038" :question/problem "Let the function $f(x,y)$ be defined as\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{y+1}{1+{x}^{2}},\\, & \\text{if}\\,1<x<2,\\,1<y<2, \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{2}}{{y}^{2}},\\, & \\text{if}\\,1<x<2,\\,2<y<4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen,\n\n$$\n{∬}_{(1,2)\\times (1,4)}f(x,y)\\text{ }\\text{d}A=\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, notice that the rectangle $R$ can be represented as a union of two smaller rectangles, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}R & =(1,2)\\times ((1,2)\\cup (2,4)) \\\\  & =((1,2)\\times (1,2))\\cup ((1,2)\\times (2,4)).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we can write\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{R}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A & ={∬}_{(1,2)\\times (1,2)}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A+{∬}_{(1,2)\\times (2,4)}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{(1,2)\\times (1,2)}\\frac{y+1}{1+{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A+{∬}_{(1,2)\\times (2,4)}\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186265807 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2e56985f-4995-5832-bc47-741b2c1c4d9c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263389} {:answer/id #uuid "2f850b1b-56d0-59fe-a9ae-66a9e18b21ff" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263400} {:answer/id #uuid "368fce54-dfc9-5cf7-bfed-6e49c5c536c5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263482} {:answer/id #uuid "5242a3d0-9686-5c9e-8bd4-51f9d91b6046" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{10}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263708} {:answer/id #uuid "a0e848e1-2f47-546b-a791-34c55dd61aef" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186264366}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186263708} :answer-field/id #uuid "7b2c7ebf-14a9-54f0-9253-98e711bf656a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186265457}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "a400e754-c19b-557c-be08-7c8b69cf6b10" :question/math-academy-id "q-130042" :question/problem "Evaluate the double integral $$\n\\displaystyle {∬}_{R}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A,\n$$ where\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}x{y}^{2},\\, & \\text{if}\\,-2<x<0,\\,0<y<2, \\\\ 2x,\\, & \\text{if}\\,0<x<1,\\,0<y<2,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand  $R$ is the domain of $f.$" :question/worked-solution "First, notice that we have $R={R}_{1}\\cup {R}_{2}$, where ${R}_{1}=(-2,0)\\times (0,2)$ and ${R}_{2}=(0,1)\\times (0,2).$ So, we can write\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{R}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A= & ={∬}_{{R}_{1}}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A+{∬}_{{R}_{2}}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{{R}_{1}}x{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}A+{∬}_{{R}_{2}}2x\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{{R}_{1}}(x)({y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}A+{∬}_{{R}_{2}}2x\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & =({\\int }_{-2}^{0}x\\,\\text{d}x)({\\int }_{0}^{2}{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}y)+(2-0){\\int }_{0}^{1}2x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={[\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}]}_{-2}^{0}\\cdot {[\\frac{{y}^{3}}{3}]}_{0}^{2}+2[{x}^{2}]_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =(-2)\\cdot \\frac{8}{3}+2 \\\\  & =-\\frac{10}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186265808 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0fabc10d-9ae1-5e94-853a-462fb1aa00c9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2{e}^{2}-e" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263139} {:answer/id #uuid "36198322-ecf6-5863-a4a5-ad9f61a2c642" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-{e}^{2}+2e-14" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263479} {:answer/id #uuid "4ec19f8f-0bbe-587e-b732-392919b19297" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2{e}^{2}-e+14" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263684} {:answer/id #uuid "5e93e174-ae19-5d17-a706-ca4ec3c6bb1f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2{e}^{2}+2e-28" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263817} {:answer/id #uuid "a416981c-bb03-5879-bcbc-2040c346ee71" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2{e}^{2}-28" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186264399}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186263817} :answer-field/id #uuid "49302468-b7d7-52f5-907e-c6fdfd044bfc" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186265385}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "a87d3263-74f6-5090-88d6-56cb978e87ed" :question/math-academy-id "q-130044" :question/problem "Evaluate the double integral $$\n\\displaystyle {∬}_{R}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A,\n$$ where\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}y{e}^{x+1},\\, & \\text{if}\\,0<x<1,\\,-2<y<0, \\\\ 3y\\sqrt{x},\\, & \\text{if}\\,1<x<4,\\,-2<y<0,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand  $R$ is the domain of $f.$" :question/worked-solution "First, notice that we have $R={R}_{1}\\cup {R}_{2}$, where ${R}_{1}=(0,1)\\times (-2,0)$ and ${R}_{2}=(1,4)\\times (-2,0).$ So, we can write\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{R}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A= & ={∬}_{{R}_{1}}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A+{∬}_{{R}_{2}}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{{R}_{1}}y{e}^{x+1}\\,\\text{d}A+{∬}_{{R}_{2}}3y\\sqrt{x}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{{R}_{1}}({e}^{x+1})(y)\\,\\text{d}A+{∬}_{{R}_{2}}(\\sqrt{x})(3y)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & =({\\int }_{0}^{1}{e}^{x+1}\\,\\text{d}x)({\\int }_{-2}^{0}y\\,\\text{d}y)+({\\int }_{1}^{4}\\sqrt{x}\\,\\text{d}x)({\\int }_{-2}^{0}3y\\,\\text{d}y) \\\\  & =[{e}^{x+1}]_{0}^{1}\\cdot {[\\frac{{y}^{2}}{2}]}_{-2}^{0}+{[\\frac{2\\sqrt{{x}^{3}}}{3}]}_{1}^{4}\\cdot {[\\frac{3{y}^{2}}{2}]}_{-2}^{0} \\\\  & =({e}^{2}-e)\\cdot (0-2)+(\\frac{16}{3}-\\frac{2}{3})\\cdot (0-6) \\\\  & =-2{e}^{2}+2e-28.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186265851 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "07bf1876-cf7e-5384-b7fe-70631d2188d3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.1084" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263071} {:answer/id #uuid "70225033-f73c-588d-a9dd-7d250c47d05c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.1275" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263969} {:answer/id #uuid "7c05e6c3-b2c4-580c-a7c8-91f0cf3eca15" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.9584" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186264071} {:answer/id #uuid "a10e63de-a04f-56eb-bb64-ebfeb3d71349" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.2584" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186264370} {:answer/id #uuid "b6fc6cb5-8cb6-5fa6-8dd4-7bd4fef22ded" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.2775" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186264585}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186264585} :answer-field/id #uuid "8ff06068-2dd1-5e69-ae32-656cb2276ffe" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186265503}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "28c8d216-a346-5e8a-b9da-a29b8df87b52" :question/math-academy-id "q-130196" :question/problem "Given $X∼\\text{Geom}(0.15),$ compute $P(X<3).$" :question/worked-solution "If $X∼\\text{Geom}(p),$ then $X$ has the following probability mass function:\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}(1-p)^{x-1}p, & x=1,2,3,… \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHere, $X∼\\text{Geom}(0.15),$ so the distribution of $X$ in this case is\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}{(0.85)}^{x-1}(0.15), & x=1,2,3,… \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X<3) & =P(X\\in \\{1,2\\}) \\\\  & =P(X=1)+P(X=2) \\\\  & =f(1)+f(2) \\\\  & ={(0.85)}^{0}(0.15)+{(0.85)}^{1}(0.15) \\\\  & =0.2775.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186265858 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "10003c18-ee89-5e47-9287-28cd8699fd93" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.0256" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263148} {:answer/id #uuid "1ed8419d-aff9-58ff-a2ff-cb0f1a80b25a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.0102" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263283} {:answer/id #uuid "a6d72351-762f-507d-89e9-a81fbe31b5e9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.9744" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186264428} {:answer/id #uuid "b3fbc289-93a4-52d1-ad45-1732fb8fb3cd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.3898" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186264546} {:answer/id #uuid "c6750a8b-1e3d-51c1-9e20-c81f216a0d38" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.9898" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186264738}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186263148} :answer-field/id #uuid "100631c3-8c6a-5bdf-880a-6dc979d7e2a0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186265273}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "48e417b8-5121-57e7-8423-7df9a5c6b9ce" :question/math-academy-id "q-130233" :question/problem "Given $X∼\\text{Geom}\\left(\\frac{3}{5}\\right),$ compute $P(X\\ge 5).$" :question/worked-solution "If $X∼\\text{Geom}(p),$ then $X$ has the following probability mass function:\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}(1-p)^{x-1}p, & x=1,2,3,… \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHere, $X∼\\text{Geom}\\left(\\frac{3}{5}\\right),$ so the distribution of $X$ in this case is\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}{\\left(\\frac{2}{5}\\right)}^{x-1}\\left(\\frac{3}{5}\\right), & x=1,2,3… \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThere are infinitely many values of $X$ such that $X\\ge 5.$ However, we can simplify the computation by using the complement instead:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X\\ge 5) & =1-P(X<5)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing the complement, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X<5) & =P(X\\in \\{1,2,3,4\\}) \\\\  & =P(X=1)+P(X=2)+P(X=3)+P(X=4) \\\\  & =f(1)+f(2)+f(3)+f(4) \\\\  & ={\\left(\\frac{2}{5}\\right)}^{0}\\left(\\frac{3}{5}\\right)+{\\left(\\frac{2}{5}\\right)}^{1}\\left(\\frac{3}{5}\\right)+{\\left(\\frac{2}{5}\\right)}^{2}\\left(\\frac{3}{5}\\right)+{\\left(\\frac{2}{5}\\right)}^{3}\\left(\\frac{3}{5}\\right) \\\\  & =0.9744.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X\\ge 5) & =1-P(X<5) \\\\  & =1-0.9744 \\\\  & =0.0256.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186265859 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "83c0ac7c-478b-5d46-ba6c-ed0eb4af934b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.0014" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186264132} {:answer/id #uuid "84e765ef-c6c9-5160-a8ac-288c0f2e2c2e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.9986" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186264141} {:answer/id #uuid "c6671d3c-c604-55f5-8e89-f9987ccaf7e5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.9959" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186264737} {:answer/id #uuid "e9f99923-aa23-5ded-93a1-029a29441b6c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.332" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186265037} {:answer/id #uuid "fa7aa329-6c14-5e61-8ffa-14c4cdf604b5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.0041" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186265194}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186265194} :answer-field/id #uuid "05963ba4-7264-5bd8-8e34-c0b81836b3f5" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186265255}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "c187994c-c002-555b-aa54-6ed7098c1e28" :question/math-academy-id "q-130236" :question/problem "Given $X∼\\text{Geom}\\left(\\frac{2}{3}\\right),$ compute $P(X\\ge 6)$ rounded to $4$ decimal places." :question/worked-solution "If $X∼\\text{Geom}(p),$ then $X$ has the following probability mass function:\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}(1-p)^{x-1}p, & x=1,2,3,… \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHere, $X∼\\text{Geom}\\left(\\frac{2}{3}\\right),$ so the distribution of $X$ in this case is\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}{\\left(\\frac{1}{3}\\right)}^{x-1}\\left(\\frac{2}{3}\\right), & x=1,2,3… \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThere are infinitely many values of $X$ such that $X\\ge 6.$ However, we can simplify the computation by using the complement instead:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X\\ge 6) & =1-P(X<6)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing the complement, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X<6) & =P(X\\in \\{1,2,3,4,5\\}) \\\\  & =P(X=1)+P(X=2)+P(X=3)+P(X=4)+P(X=5) \\\\  & =f(1)+f(2)+f(3)+f(4)+f(5) \\\\  & ={\\left(\\frac{1}{3}\\right)}^{0}\\left(\\frac{2}{3}\\right)+{\\left(\\frac{1}{3}\\right)}^{1}\\left(\\frac{2}{3}\\right)+{\\left(\\frac{1}{3}\\right)}^{2}\\left(\\frac{2}{3}\\right)+{\\left(\\frac{1}{3}\\right)}^{3}\\left(\\frac{2}{3}\\right)+{\\left(\\frac{1}{3}\\right)}^{4}\\left(\\frac{2}{3}\\right) \\\\  & ≈0.9959.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X\\ge 6) & =1-P(X<6) \\\\  & ≈1-0.9959 \\\\  & =0.0041.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186266042 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "53a22dd3-a12d-5858-8abe-50ea411d89b9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186463964} {:answer/id #uuid "36046449-1599-5cbe-a388-6f9f7a5cb79c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186463965}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186463964} :answer-field/id #uuid "d07414d7-38bb-5a41-a70a-cab1a7c6266e" :answer-field/key "$[-\\frac{\\pi }{3},\\frac{\\pi }{3}]$" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186464916} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6ef93042-d8eb-5786-ae85-d1a2674d5ca7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186463966} {:answer/id #uuid "7809c3f4-194e-508e-aa57-63f005ea71e3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186463967}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186463967} :answer-field/id #uuid "b0621149-4a49-5264-943b-8b19e5114ba8" :answer-field/key "$[\\frac{\\pi }{6},\\frac{\\pi }{3}]$" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186464917} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "cead2490-5a8f-5617-b718-96a31861ea17" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186463968} {:answer/id #uuid "44cc7066-d7c7-5444-8303-dac86b7c407b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186463969}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186463968} :answer-field/id #uuid "372a4dc1-c392-5350-a730-58b07fa01c7e" :answer-field/key "$[\\frac{\\pi }{3},\\frac{2\\pi }{3}]$" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186464918}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "d91387a3-da34-5d28-af8d-36338cffabf0" :question/math-academy-id "q-131066" :question/problem "Suppose that $f(x)$ is defined as\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}2, & x<0 \\\\ \\operatorname{tan}⁡x, & x\\ge 0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nOn which of the following intervals can the extreme value theorem be applied to the function?\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "Remember that the extreme value theorem is only applicable to closed intervals on which the function is continuous.\n\nSo, let's check whether the function is continuous on each of the intervals.\n\n- Consider $[-\\frac{\\pi }{3},\\frac{\\pi }{3}].$ We have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to {0}^{-}}f(x) & =\\lim_{x\\to 0}2=2, \\\\ \\lim_{x\\to {0}^{+}}f(x) & =\\lim_{x\\to 0}\\operatorname{tan}⁡x=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the limits from the left and from the right of $x=0$ are not the same. Hence, $f(x)$ has a discontinuity at $x=0.$ Therefore, $f(x)$ is not continuous on the interval $[-\\frac{\\pi }{3},\\frac{\\pi }{3}],$ and the extreme value theorem is not applicable on this interval.\n\n- Consider $[\\frac{\\pi }{6},\\frac{\\pi }{3}].$ In this interval, $f(x)=\\operatorname{tan}⁡x,$ so the function $f(x)$ is continuous on the interval $[\\frac{\\pi }{6},\\frac{\\pi }{3}],$ and the extreme value theorem is applicable on this interval.\n\n- Consider $[\\frac{\\pi }{3},\\frac{2\\pi }{3}].$ In this interval, $f(x)=\\operatorname{tan}⁡x,$ so the function is not defined at $x=\\frac{\\pi }{2}.$ Therefore, $f(x)$ is not continuous on the interval $[\\frac{\\pi }{3},\\frac{2\\pi }{3}],$ and the extreme value theorem is not applicable on this interval.\n\nIn conclusion, the correct answer is $[\\frac{\\pi }{6},\\frac{\\pi }{3}]$ only."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186266108 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1d071ed6-2702-5a8c-a1bb-c6115d93069c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263265} {:answer/id #uuid "22018c6a-c000-55d9-ad50-bbf2d8dc310a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{3}{25}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263300} {:answer/id #uuid "2aa73e37-f65f-5473-8506-1046bb0408e8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-15" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263365} {:answer/id #uuid "534b38df-3041-5cc5-bf51-997cf44692b8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "30" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263717} {:answer/id #uuid "5cbef439-267b-552e-bccf-b3b24da20aea" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{14}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263802}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186263365} :answer-field/id #uuid "c1c8fd0b-22e6-58f9-911b-2b7e540386c7" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186265585}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "78d3c6b5-0dc7-5dd1-a65f-4624387da0ab" :question/math-academy-id "q-131654" :question/problem "Find \n$$\n\\displaystyle \\begin{vmatrix}r & 1 & 0 \\\\ 5 & 3t & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -25\\end{vmatrix}\n$$\ngiven that \n$$\n\\displaystyle \\begin{vmatrix}r & 1 \\\\ 5 & 3t\\end{vmatrix}=\\frac{3}{5}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Notice that we have a block diagonal matrix. Therefore, its determinant is equal to the product of the diagonal blocks' determinants:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{vmatrix}r & 1 & 0 \\\\ 5 & 3t & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -25\\end{vmatrix} & =|\\begin{array}{cc|c}r & 1 & 0 \\\\ 5 & 3t & 0 \\\\ \\hline 0 & 0 & -25\\end{array}| \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}r & 1 \\\\ 5 & 3t\\end{vmatrix}\\cdot (-25) \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{5}\\cdot (-25) \\\\  & =-15.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186266109 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "62b803d7-a384-5ae1-abf3-11a9999dc13b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263852} {:answer/id #uuid "698da396-7f02-58f4-a1a3-ec217a1dffd1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{7}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263906} {:answer/id #uuid "920ae89c-0ba8-5baa-92ac-2fc082d1e962" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "49" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186264251} {:answer/id #uuid "ba2b38b1-b5a2-5ab3-843e-999d444de5f7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{24}{7}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186264613} {:answer/id #uuid "d058e104-fc83-5a89-b0d3-3dd17a691395" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186264824}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186264824} :answer-field/id #uuid "70f30f2e-2647-56fd-9734-06d501f09310" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186265438}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "7bdfaa5e-09b0-53c0-aa6b-c7cff92a4a13" :question/math-academy-id "q-131675" :question/problem "Find $$\n\\displaystyle \\,\\begin{vmatrix}v & 5{w}^{2} & 0 & 0 \\\\ 3v & 2 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 3 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -8 & 5\\end{vmatrix}\n$$ given that $$\n\\displaystyle \\,\\begin{vmatrix}v & 5{w}^{2} \\\\ 3v & 2\\end{vmatrix}=\\frac{1}{7}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Notice that we have a block diagonal matrix. Therefore, its determinant is equal to the product of the diagonal blocks' determinants:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{vmatrix}v & 5{w}^{2} & 0 & 0 \\\\ 3v & 2 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 3 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -8 & 5\\end{vmatrix} & =|\\begin{array}{cc|cc}v & 5{w}^{2} & 0 & 0 \\\\ 3v & 2 & 0 & 0 \\\\ \\hline 0 & 0 & 3 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -8 & 5\\end{array}| \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}v & 5{w}^{2} \\\\ 3v & 2\\end{vmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}3 & -1 \\\\ -8 & 5\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{7}\\cdot 7 \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186271990 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1aee602c-4d2a-536f-b5a1-43a412811ff2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186464072} {:answer/id #uuid "13c7384c-1cfa-57cc-8a50-9bf3eab8a9e2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186464073}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186464072} :answer-field/id #uuid "7c3b7373-e5a8-5e2b-8acc-0be10c9b59f2" :answer-field/key "$-3|\\begin{aligned}k & 3 \\\\ 7 & -2\\end{aligned}|=|\\begin{aligned}k & 1 \\\\ 7 & 8\\end{aligned}|$" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186464970} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "550ef764-b469-5976-ae9d-0020002e1ead" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186464074} {:answer/id #uuid "d1697d54-6fd0-5d05-b11b-25f7ce8acc26" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186464075}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186464075} :answer-field/id #uuid "1f5b3e10-e84c-56fd-8992-d584ea10b2ad" :answer-field/key "$3|\\begin{aligned}k & 3 \\\\ 7 & -2\\end{aligned}|=|\\begin{aligned}k & 1 \\\\ 7 & 8\\end{aligned}|$" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186464971} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "fab9ce83-630e-5c19-ae07-249274247140" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186464076} {:answer/id #uuid "7bc5ed4c-a4d9-52b6-ad58-1348db969598" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186464077}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186464076} :answer-field/id #uuid "5b13ee4f-81c8-5307-88d8-1a4665b341de" :answer-field/key "$|\\begin{aligned}k & 3 \\\\ 7 & -2\\end{aligned}|=3|\\begin{aligned}k & 1 \\\\ 7 & 8\\end{aligned}|$" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186464972}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "09aa5cfb-0ccb-582a-bfa8-b6151a1b73a2" :question/math-academy-id "q-139979" :question/problem "Let $⟨x,3⟩$ be the solution of the system of equations below. \n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}kx+3y &=1 \\\\ 7x-2y &=8.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nWhich of the following equations could be solved to find the value of $k?$\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "The determinant of the coefficient matrix is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}D & =\\begin{vmatrix}k & 3 \\\\ 7 & -2\\end{vmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAccording to Cramer's rule, the value of $y$ is given by $y=\\frac{{D}_{y}}{D},$ where\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{D}_{y} & =\\begin{vmatrix}k & 1 \\\\ 7 & 8\\end{vmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =\\frac{{D}_{y}}{D} \\\\ 3 & =\\frac{\\begin{vmatrix}k & 1 \\\\ 7 & 8\\end{vmatrix}}{\\begin{vmatrix}k & 3 \\\\ 7 & -2\\end{vmatrix}} \\\\ 3\\begin{vmatrix}k & 3 \\\\ 7 & -2\\end{vmatrix} & =\\begin{vmatrix}k & 1 \\\\ 7 & 8\\end{vmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the correct answer is $$\n\\displaystyle 3\\begin{vmatrix}k & 3 \\\\ 7 & -2\\end{vmatrix}=\\begin{vmatrix}k & 1 \\\\ 7 & 8\\end{vmatrix}\n$$ only."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186271991 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8504eb0f-93c6-5d20-8195-88dbad00b315" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186464078} {:answer/id #uuid "c70ffc2d-dbef-5e28-8e5a-6e4a97b209b2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186464079}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186464078} :answer-field/id #uuid "83c98712-e70e-5484-9096-216f3da1b3f6" :answer-field/key "$|\\begin{aligned}15 & 8 \\\\ k & 13\\end{aligned}|=2|\\begin{aligned}15 & -1 \\\\ k & -31\\end{aligned}|$" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186464973} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "855711cd-7b19-555c-ab6e-e3cbe95bee72" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186464080} {:answer/id #uuid "8f8516b4-64ce-53f5-938e-8dfecd51f520" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186464081}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186464080} :answer-field/id #uuid "acfad26a-36f9-5d0e-a1e7-36cd998ca21e" :answer-field/key "$|\\begin{aligned}15 & 8 \\\\ k & 13\\end{aligned}|=-4|\\begin{aligned}15 & -1 \\\\ k & -31\\end{aligned}|$" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186464974} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "988ccea1-2f9c-50fe-b323-1720447bfb02" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186464082} {:answer/id #uuid "725b0b06-d3c5-567b-9157-156746df62ed" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186464083}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186464083} :answer-field/id #uuid "535c6a07-6be3-5ec1-a653-291a4880438d" :answer-field/key "$-2|\\begin{aligned}15 & 8 \\\\ k & 13\\end{aligned}|=|\\begin{aligned}15 & -1 \\\\ k & -31\\end{aligned}|$" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186464975}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "ddc658b6-e0a7-590f-af72-c80671fc917f" :question/math-academy-id "q-139980" :question/problem "Let $⟨x,-2⟩$ be the solution of the system of equations below. \n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}15x+8y &=-1 \\\\ kx+13y &=-31.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nWhich of the following equations could be solved to find the value of $k?$\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "The determinant of the coefficient matrix is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}D & =\\begin{vmatrix}15 & 8 \\\\ k & 13\\end{vmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAccording to Cramer's rule, the value of $y$ is given by $y=\\frac{{D}_{y}}{D},$ where\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{D}_{y} & =\\begin{vmatrix}15 & -1 \\\\ k & -31\\end{vmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =\\frac{{D}_{y}}{D} \\\\ -2 & =\\frac{\\begin{vmatrix}15 & -1 \\\\ k & -31\\end{vmatrix}}{\\begin{vmatrix}15 & 8 \\\\ k & 13\\end{vmatrix}} \\\\ -2\\begin{vmatrix}15 & 8 \\\\ k & 13\\end{vmatrix} & =\\begin{vmatrix}15 & -1 \\\\ k & -31\\end{vmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the correct answer is $$\n\\displaystyle -2\\begin{vmatrix}15 & 8 \\\\ k & 13\\end{vmatrix}=\\begin{vmatrix}15 & -1 \\\\ k & -31\\end{vmatrix}\n$$ only."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186272021 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "afa82d44-d03d-5e9b-af04-272ab90230e5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186464096} {:answer/id #uuid "71f897c8-dfce-5b45-8f4c-bb7062ae467e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186464097}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186464096} :answer-field/id #uuid "6ed222a1-7190-5964-99bc-a9de5fd0be28" :answer-field/key "$\\text{Ker}(T)\\ne \\{0\\}$" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186464982} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5a1a3dca-d308-5f38-927f-67b0c977e32e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186464098} {:answer/id #uuid "d292b59f-adc1-58d1-98bc-5da647707c1a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186464099}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186464099} :answer-field/id #uuid "cafebd75-e7fa-54e1-9bcf-2578d3061be4" :answer-field/key "$T$ is invertible" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186464983} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "dbb177f8-879b-5898-9551-53329abe91c3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186464100} {:answer/id #uuid "7332d203-8792-51a5-976c-10e58a391aaf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186464101}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186464101} :answer-field/id #uuid "b8de8dc2-3957-5b43-9b1f-bfdfb462141b" :answer-field/key "$\\text{nullity}(T)=0$" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186464984}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "1cac76f2-abeb-58d4-87e9-5f3619bedd4c" :question/math-academy-id "q-140085" :question/problem "Let $T:{R}^{5}\\to {R}^{5}$ be a linear transformation whose standard matrix is $T.$ Consider the following statement:\n\n*If $\\text{rank}(T)=5,$ then $\\underline{\\phantom{xxxxxx}}.$*\n\nWhich of the following can be placed into the blank space to make a true statement?\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "Since $T$ is a linear transformation from ${R}^{5}$ into ${R}^{5},$ the invertible matrix theorem states the following:\n\n $T$ is a bijection (an invertible transformation) $$\n\\displaystyle \\,⟺\\,\\{\\,\\begin{aligned} & \\text{Im}(T)={R}^{5} \\\\  & \\text{rank}(T)=5 \\\\  & \\text{Ker}(T)=\\{0\\} \\\\  & \\text{nullity}(T)=0\\end{aligned}\n$$ \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement $\\text{Ker}(T)\\ne \\{0\\}$ is false. Since $\\text{rank}(T)=5,$ the invertible matrix theorem implies that $\\text{Ker}(T)=\\{0\\}.$\n\n- Statement “$T$ is invertible” is true. Indeed, since $\\text{rank}(T)=5,$ the invertible matrix theorem implies that $T$ is invertible.\n\n- Statement $\\text{nullity}(T)=0$ is true. Indeed, since $T$ is invertible, the invertible matrix theorem implies that $\\text{nullity}(T)=0.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is “$T$ is invertible”; $\\text{nullity}(T)=0$ only."}]}
